window.NORD_SEARCH=[{"f":"welcome.html","t":"Welcome","s":"Manual","x":"Nord Engine is a personal engine plus a native editor. You build a game or app visually (hierarchy, inspector, viewport), script it in JavaScript (or Rust), and export a static folder that runs in any browser — or wrap it into a Mac app. For dummies Think of Nord as a workshop. The editor is the workbench where you arrange things. The runtime is the little engine that makes your game move when it is played. Export packs everything into one folder you can put on any website. The tiers # You pick a tier per project ( type in nord.project.json ). All tiers share the same project folder layout and scene format. Tier type Runs on Use it for HTML html your own HTML/JS (+ optional nord-lite.js helpers) small games, tools, apps with plain DOM/canvas 2D 2d Phaser 4 (Arcade or Matter physics) platformers, shooters, puzzle, top-down 3D 3d Babylon.js 9 + Havok physics first-person, racing, 3D platformers Rust game wasm-game macroquad + rapier2d compiled to WebAssembly CPU-heavy 2D sims, Rust-only logic Rust app wasm-app eframe/egui compiled to WebAssembly tools, dashboards, native-feeling apps nord doctor checks your toolchain and recommends a tier. You never write Phaser or Babylon code: scripts use Nord&#39;s own API ( NordScript , entity.* , game.* ). Raw engine objects are only reachable through the entity.native escape hatch. Install and first launch (macOS) # Build or open the app: ./build.sh produces the editor .app ; ./run.sh [project] runs it from source. A splash screen shows for at least two seconds, then the project hub opens: New (template picker), Open , Recent , Examples , Tutorials . Pick a template (for example Platformer ) and press ▶ Play (Cmd+R). Command line (any OS): Tip The whole manual is offline and also lives inside the editor&#39;s Docs tab. Press / anywhere on this site to search. How this manual is organised # Manual — chapters 1–17, read in order or jump around. Tutorials — T01–T18, each builds something and points to a runnable project. Guides — cheat sheets, cookbook, glossary, shortcuts. API / Components / Reference — generated from the source, so they cannot drift: JS classes, every component, the CLI, the library catalog. For dummies Every concept page can hide a purple For dummies box like this one. Flip the switch in the top bar if you want plain-language explanations and analogies; leave it off once you know the ropes."},{"f":"editor-tour.html","t":"Editor tour","s":"Manual","x":"The editor is a native egui app. Its layout follows the PlayCanvas editor with Defold-style toolbars. Appearance and project hub # The default Graphite theme uses neutral dark-grey panels, a darker canvas and a soft blue accent. Existing settings using the original Nord default migrate once; custom themes remain selected. All previous themes are available in Settings. The blue Play-mode tint and your chosen PLAYMODE-COLOR are preserved. The hub provides New project, Recent projects and searchable Examples/Tutorials in separately scrolling cards. Expand Starter templates for a ready-made project; the templates are included in the app bundle. Hover a recent-project row to see its full path. Docs opens the guides even before opening a project; Back to projects returns to the hub. Toolbar Play toggles Stop, Export… opens the export dialog, and the search icon opens the command palette. Settings scrolls in smaller windows. Drag dock tabs to rearrange panels; closed tabs reopen from View. Panels # Hierarchy — the entity tree ( Add entity button; right-click: Add child, Duplicate, Delete, Copy, Paste). Drag a row onto another to reparent (top 30 % of a row inserts before ), drop on empty space to move to the root. Dropping an asset on empty space creates an entity from it. Viewport — Scene tab is the edit view (2D Canvas / 3D Babylon): pan, orbit, zoom, pick, and move/rotate/scale gizmos with snapping. Game tab runs the real game. Inspector — generated from component schemas and script static attributes . Add components with + Add component and scripts with + Add script ; change values live. Assets — folder tree plus thumbnail grid (images, spritesheets, audio waveforms, rendered models). Import by dropping files from the OS. Library — search ready-made scripts, prefabs and kits; Insert adds them to the project/selection. Console — console.* output of the running game and build output. Click file.js:12 to open that line. Code — a native code editor with tabs, highlighting (10 languages), find/replace (regex), project search, autocomplete (nord.js API + library + file words), error squiggles, minimap, split view and multi-cursor. Docs — this manual. Play, stop, reload # ▶ Play (Cmd+R) runs the game in the Game tab with live reload: saving a script hot-reloads it without restarting the scene (see Scripting ). ■ Stop is Cmd+ . . For Rust projects Play builds first and streams cargo output to the Console. Undo and project safety # Every scene change is an undoable command (Cmd+Z / Shift+Cmd+Z). The editor autosaves with crash recovery; Cmd+S saves explicitly. For dummies Don&#39;t be afraid to click around: almost everything has undo. The two places you will spend 90 % of your time are the Hierarchy (what exists) and the Inspector (how each thing is set up). Shortcuts # See the shortcut table (also in Settings, Cmd+,). Highlights: Cmd+P quick open, Cmd+K command palette, W/E/R gizmos, F focus, Cmd+D duplicate. Themes and skins # Settings → Skin (fonts) and Theme (colours). The same skins/themes exist for in-game UI ( project.ui.skin / ui.theme , see UI ). Current example content # Settings → USE CACHED CONTENT is OFF by default . Examples and Tutorials open the current files from your checkout or rebuilt app bundle. Old Recent entries for saved example copies also resolve to the current bundled example. Save edits to a separate project if you want your own version: editing an example with this setting OFF changes its current source files. Turn the setting ON to test saved example content: the editor creates/reuses a private copy under ~/.config/nord/examples/ . Rebuilding the app leaves those copies as they were. Reopen the example after changing this setting to switch its source. OFF also uses temporary embedded-browser storage and reads script attributes fresh. ON permits persistent embedded-browser storage. Changing the mode recreates webviews; game saves in temporary browser storage last only for that view. The dev server always reads requested files from disk and sends Cache-Control: no-store . Where the editor saves files # The editor&#39;s settings folder contains saved example/tutorial copies as well as your preferences. Rebuilding or replacing the app does not remove this folder. NORD_CONFIG_DIR , when set before launching the editor, overrides the entire folder on every platform. Platform Settings folder without NORD_CONFIG_DIR Saved example/tutorial copies macOS $HOME/.config/nord/ , normally /Users/ user /.config/nord/ /Users/ user /.config/nord/examples/ example-name / Linux $HOME/.config/nord/ , normally /home/ user /.config/nord/ /home/ user /.config/nord/examples/ example-name / Windows, with HOME set %HOME%\\.config\\nord\\ %HOME%\\.config\\nord\\examples\\ example-name \\ Windows, with HOME unset .config\\nord\\ under the editor&#39;s launch working directory .config\\nord\\examples\\ example-name \\ under that directory The current editor does not automatically select Windows %APPDATA% or %USERPROFILE% , macOS ~/Library/Application Support , or Linux $XDG_CONFIG_HOME . For a predictable Windows location, set NORD_CONFIG_DIR to an absolute path such as C:\\Users\\ user \\AppData\\Roaming\\NordEngine before launching the editor. If HOME is unset on macOS or Linux, the same working-directory fallback applies. Inside the settings folder: File or folder Contents examples/ example-name / Complete saved project copies, including sprites, textures and scripts. Created/reused when USE CACHED CONTENT is ON; old copies can remain from earlier editor versions. editor.json Editor settings, including use_cached_content . layout.json Panel layout. recent.json Recent project paths. On macOS, open Finder → Go → Go to Folder… ( Cmd+Shift+G ) and enter ~/.config/nord/ . On Linux, enter ~/.config/nord/ in your file manager; Ctrl+H usually shows hidden folders. On Windows, paste the resolved path into File Explorer&#39;s address bar. Every opened project can also contain these folders, on all three platforms: Project-r"},{"f":"projects-scenes.html","t":"Projects & scenes","s":"Manual","x":"A project is a folder of plain files — everything is JSON, scripts and assets, so it works with git and any text editor. The complete field reference is in Project format . This chapter explains the ideas. Entities # An entity has a name, id, tags, a layer, a transform (position, rotation in degrees, scale), components and scripts , and may have children . Child transforms are relative to the parent. Units: 2D = pixels, y down; 3D = metres, y up, left-handed. For dummies An entity is an empty box. On its own it does nothing. You make it a player by putting a sprite component (what it looks like), a rigidbody (it obeys physics) and a script (what it does) into the box. Components # Components give an entity data and behaviour: sprite , rigidbody , light , camera … The list is in the component index . They are plain JSON in the scene: { type : sprite , texture : assets/sprites/hero.png } . Scripts # Scripts are JavaScript modules attached to entities ( scripts : [{ path : scripts/player.js , attributes : { speed : 220 } }] ). See Scripting . A script&#39;s static attributes show up in the inspector. Tags and layers # tags are free-form labels: entity.hasTag(&#39;enemy&#39;) , scene.findByTag(&#39;coin&#39;) . layer is an integer used for ordering/filtering by the backend. Prefabs # A prefab is a saved entity (with children). Scenes reference it with prefab : { path : prefabs/coin.prefab.json , overrides : {…} } . overrides is deep-merged onto the prefab root; an object with numeric keys patches array items by index ( { components : { 0 : { tint : #f00 }}} ). Instance fields ( name , transform , tags , enabled ) win over the prefab&#39;s. At runtime: await this.scene.spawn(&#39;prefabs/bullet.prefab.json&#39;, { pos: [x, y, 0], overrides: {…} }) . Scenes # A scene is one JSON file. game.loadScene(path) swaps scenes; the start scene is startScene in the project file. Scene settings hold ambient colour, skybox and gravity overrides. Tip Hand-editing JSON is fine — nord check validates ids, tags and missing files, and the editor shows the same problems."},{"f":"scripting.html","t":"Scripting","s":"Manual","x":"Scripts are ES modules whose default export extends NordScript . The one import is nord : For dummies A script is a list of instructions glued onto an entity. The engine calls your functions at the right moments ( start when the level begins, update every picture/frame, …). dt is how many seconds since the last frame — multiply by it so things move at the same speed on slow and fast computers. Lifecycle # Hook When init() once, after construction, before any start() start() once, when the scene begins (all entities exist). Spawned entities start on the next frame update(dt) every frame, seconds fixedUpdate(dt) fixed 60 Hz — use for physics-related logic lateUpdate(dt) after every update (cameras, followers) onEnable() / onDisable() entity enabled / disabled onDestroy() entity destroyed onCollisionEnter(other, contact) / onCollisionExit(other) solid contact ( other is an Entity) onTriggerEnter(other) / onTriggerExit(other) overlap with a trigger / trigger collider onHotReload(oldState) after the file was hot-swapped An exception in any hook is caught, shown in the Console (and an on-screen overlay) and the game keeps running. Attributes # static attributes declares inspector-editable parameters. Types: number, int, bool, string, color, vec2, vec3, enum (options), entity, asset (assetType), curve, array . Values from the scene are validated, clamped to min / max , and unknown keys warn. Read them from this.attrs . Full table: Project format → attribute types . The entity API # this.entity has: name, id, tags, enabled, parent, children, pos, rot, scale, worldPos , find(name) , findByTag(tag) , hasTag , getComponent(type) , addComponent(type, data) , set(type, key, value) , getScript(Class) , addScript , on/off/emit , destroy() , clone() , and native (escape hatch). Reference: Entity . Position arrays can be replaced ( e.pos = [x, y, 0] , syncs automatically) or edited in place followed by e.touch() . Events # Two buses: the global one ( this.events / game.events ) and a per-entity one ( entity.on/emit ). Handlers are removed with the function returned by on . Timers and tweens # this.after(sec, fn) , this.every(sec, fn) and this.tween(target, to, opts) all return a cancel function and respect game.timeScale . Easing names: see Math helpers . Spawning and finding # Hot reload # When you save a .js script during Play (or via nord serve ), Nord re-imports it with a cache-buster and swaps the prototype of every live instance , keeping its fields. Attributes are re-resolved against the new class. Define onHotReload(oldState) if you need to rebuild something (for example re-create DOM). Imports of the script (helper modules) are not re-fetched — reload the page for those. A syntax error leaves the game untouched and shows the error. Debugging # console.log in a game shows up in the editor Console with a clickable file.js:line . Errors show on screen (red overlay at the bottom; click to dismiss) and in game.errors . The library has util-debug.js (FPS/entity counters) — see the catalog . Use game.timeScale = 0.1 to watch fast things; game.pause() to freeze. Scripts in Rust # The Rust tiers use a Script trait instead — see Rust / WASM ."},{"f":"2d.html","t":"2D games","s":"Manual","x":"2D projects ( type : 2d ) run on Phaser 4. You describe things with Nord components; the backend creates the Phaser objects. Project display settings: display.width/height (the logical resolution), scale ( fit ), pixelArt (nearest-neighbour filtering), background . For dummies In 2D everything is a flat picture placed on a big sheet. x goes right, y goes down (the opposite of school maths), and the numbers are pixels. Sprites # Add a sprite with a texture . For sheets, add a sidecar assets/hero.png.meta.json : Use animatedSprite and getComponent(&#39;animatedSprite&#39;).play(&#39;run&#39;) . Flip with setFlip(true) ; tint with entity.set(&#39;animatedSprite&#39;, &#39;tint&#39;, &#39;#f66&#39;) . Tilemaps # tilemap loads a Tiled JSON map or a Nord simple map ( { tileWidth, tileHeight, layers: [{ data: [[…]] }], collides: [...] } ). Tiles listed in collides are solid for physics. Tilemaps live in world space and are static — position the entity before the scene starts. Edit tiles at runtime with setTile(tx, ty, index) ; the editor has a tilemap painter. Camera # The camera component configures the main camera: follow an entity by name/id, lerp smoothing, bounds , deadzone , zoom . Effects live on game.camera : shake , flash , fade , zoom , center (see game.* namespaces ). Parallax backgrounds # Several parallax entities with different factor s and Z (depth) values give depth. Put far layers at lower z. Particles # particles : continuous ( frequency , quantity ) or one-shot ( burst ). Trigger bursts from code: getComponent(&#39;particles&#39;).burst(20) . Library presets: fx-particle-presets.js (explosion, smoke, sparkle…). Lights # Adding a light turns on Phaser&#39;s light pipeline for sprites, tilemaps and parallax. Use ambient in scene settings for the base darkness. Great for torches ( flicker ). Text # World-space text for signs and damage numbers; for HUD use uiLabel (DOM, always crisp). Fonts: see UI . Juice checklist # game.camera.shake() on hits, fx-hitstop.js for impact freeze. this.tween(entity.scale, [1.2, 1.2, 1], { duration: 0.1, yoyo: true }) for pops. Particles on pickups, a sound for everything ( Audio ). Pixel-art tips # Set display.pixelArt: true and use a small logical resolution (320×180 or 640×360); scale: fit letterboxes it to the window. Keep sprite sizes multiples of the grid and use filter: nearest in meta files."},{"f":"3d.html","t":"3D games","s":"Manual","x":"3D projects ( type : 3d ) run on Babylon.js with Havok physics. Units are metres, y up, left-handed ; rotation is Euler degrees (XYZ). For dummies 3D is the same entity-and-component idea, plus a third direction: y is up , z is forward . A mesh is the shape, a material is its paint, a light lets you see it. Building blocks # mesh — box, sphere, cylinder, capsule, torus, plane, ground. Perfect for greyboxing. model — a glTF/GLB. Models are instanced; animations are named clips ( play(&#39;walk&#39;) ). material — PBR: color , metallic , roughness , textures, emissive , alpha , unlit . light — directional (sun), point, spot, hemispheric (ambient sky). castShadows on a directional/spot light enables shadows for everything with castShadow . skybox — cube map folder ( assets/skybox/day , sunset , night ship with the engine). postProcess — bloom, FXAA/MSAA, vignette, tonemap, grain. particles — Babylon particle system with the same component name as 2D. Cameras # A camera entity creates a Babylon camera: type: orbit (follows follow , optional mouse control) or a free camera attached to the entity (first person: put the camera as a child of the player body). Switch with getComponent(&#39;camera&#39;).activate() . Library: fps3d.js , third-person3d.js , orbit-cam.js , fly-cam.js , rts-cam.js . Animation # model.play(name, { loop, speed }) . Clip names come from the glTF; getComponent(&#39;model&#39;).animations lists them. Blend by switching clips with short tweens on speed if you need to. The Kenney pack in assets/packs/kenney-platformer-3d/ has a rigged character to experiment with ( Assets ). 3D UI # Use the DOM layer ( game.ui , see UI ) for HUDs. For labels floating above entities, game.gui.worldLabel(entity, &#39;Bob&#39;) (Babylon GUI). Picking and rays # game.physics.raycast(from, to, { ignore }) returns the first physics hit with the entity. The library has interact-ray.js and physics-hitscan.js . Performance tips # Reuse materials and models (instancing is automatic), keep shadow map size modest (1024 default), prefer one directional light with shadows, and use postProcess sparingly on laptops. See Performance ."},{"f":"physics.html","t":"Physics","s":"Manual","x":"Physics is selected in nord.project.json : arcade (default for 2D; fast, AABB), matter (2D; rotation, polygons, constraints), havok (3D; automatic for 3d ). 3D gravity default is [0, -9.81, 0] (m/s²); 2D gravity is px/s². The backend is stepped from the fixed 60 Hz loop , in lock-step with fixedUpdate . For dummies Physics = the rules of the world: things fall, bump, and stop. Give an entity a rigidbody to say obey the rules and a collider to say this is my shape . Static bodies (floors, walls) never move. Dynamic bodies move. Kinematic bodies are moved by you . Setting up a body # No collider size → taken from the sprite/mesh bounds. A body without a rigidbody is static. Details: rigidbody , collider , trigger . Moving things # Assigning entity.pos teleports the body (velocity is zeroed). Rotating a 3D body keeps its velocity so yaw/steering works. Events # onCollisionEnter(other, contact) , onCollisionExit(other) , onTriggerEnter(other) , onTriggerExit(other) . Entities also emit collisionEnter , triggerEnter … on their entity bus. In 3D, contact is { point, normal, impulse } . Queries # 2D: physics.overlapBox(x, y, w, h) → entities (and {tile:true} markers for solid tiles). 3D: physics.raycast(from, to, { ignore }) → { entity, point, normal, distance } . isGrounded(entity) for both. Recipes (library) # physics-moving-platform , physics-one-way , physics-bounce-pad , physics-conveyor , physics-crate , physics-door , physics-drag , physics-explosion , physics-knockback , physics-magnet , physics-projectile , physics-rope , physics-buoyancy , physics-trigger-zone … see the catalog . Careful In 3D, Havok isGrounded / raycast with ignore only skips the first hit (Havok returns the closest hit), and script-set transforms zero a dynamic body&#39;s velocity (rotation does not). Havok needs its .wasm served as application/wasm (see Hosting )."},{"f":"audio.html","t":"Audio","s":"Manual","x":"this.audio is a WebAudio manager with four buses : master → music / sfx / ui . Calls made before the player&#39;s first click/key are queued and replayed (browsers block audio until a gesture). Missing or blocked audio never throws into game code. For dummies Think of buses as volume knobs: one knob for music, one for effects, one for menus, and a big master knob over all of them. bus option: &#39;sfx&#39; (default), &#39;music&#39; , &#39;ui&#39; . Volumes and mutes are saved per game in localStorage and restored on start. Preload with this.audio.preload([...paths]) to avoid first-play latency. The audioSource component attaches a clip to an entity ( autoplay , loop , bus ) and exposes play() / stop() . UI sounds click and hover ( assets/audio/sfx/click.wav , hover.wav ) play automatically for game.ui buttons. Library: util-sfx.js (event-name → sound map via events.emit(&#39;sfx&#39;, &#39;hit&#39;) ), ui-settings.js (volume sliders). Tip MP3 adds ~25 ms of encoder delay, so use OGG or WAV for gapless loops. The Rust tier supports WAV/OGG only. Audio is not spatialised yet ( audioListener is a placeholder)."},{"f":"ui.html","t":"UI & HUD","s":"Manual","x":"game.ui is a lightweight DOM overlay on top of the canvas — crisp text, accessible, CSS-themed. It works the same in 2D, 3D and plain HTML projects. Widgets live on named screens (layers). hud always exists and is visible; create others for menus and show them on demand. For dummies Imagine a sheet of see-through plastic laid over the game. You draw the score, buttons and menus on that sheet, and the game keeps running underneath. Positioning options for every widget: x, y, anchor (&#39;tl&#39;|&#39;tr&#39;|&#39;bl&#39;|&#39;br&#39;), w, h, screen, style . Widgets: panel , label , button , image , bar , menu . Handles have el (the DOM node) and remove() ; labels and bars add set() . UI as components # uiPanel , uiLabel , uiImage , uiBar , uiButton put the same widgets into the scene file so the editor can lay them out . A uiButton with event: start-game emits that name on game.events when clicked. Themes and skins # Project-level: ui : { skin : cyber , theme : tokyo-night } . A skin picks fonts, a theme picks colours ( --nord-* CSS variables). At runtime: ui.setTheme({ theme: &#39;dracula&#39;, skin: &#39;cyber&#39; }) or tweak single variables with ui.theme({ accent: &#39;#ebcb8b&#39; }) . The catalogue is in assets/themes/themes.json (editor and game share it). Fonts come from vendor/fonts — nothing is loaded from the internet. Ready-made screens (library) # ui-hud , ui-main-menu , ui-pause , ui-gameover , ui-settings , ui-level-select , ui-loading , ui-toasts , ui-damage-numbers , ui-inventory , ui-joystick . See the catalog . Touch controls # virtualJoystick(input, parent) (or the ui-joystick library script) drives your four direction actions and input.stick , so the same axis / vec code works on phones. In-world UI # 2D: text component or damage-number script. 3D: game.gui.worldLabel(entity, &#39;text&#39;) follows an entity in screen space (Babylon GUI). Apps # For tool/app projects the library has an app kit ( library/apps/_kit.js : layout, forms, lists, router, state) and eight sample apps — see Templates examples ."},{"f":"input.html","t":"Input","s":"Manual","x":"Scripts never read raw keys. You declare actions in the project and ask about the action: For dummies An action is a job name ( jump ) that several buttons can trigger. Your code says was jump pressed? and doesn&#39;t care whether it was the spacebar or a gamepad button — so players can remap keys without you changing the game. Call Meaning input.down(&#39;jump&#39;) held this frame input.pressed(&#39;jump&#39;) went down this frame input.released(&#39;jump&#39;) went up this frame input.axis(&#39;left&#39;,&#39;right&#39;) −1, 0 or 1 input.vec(&#39;left&#39;,&#39;right&#39;,&#39;up&#39;,&#39;down&#39;) normalised 2D vector (analog stick wins when pushed) input.key(&#39;KeyW&#39;) raw key code (debug) input.pointer { x, y, worldX, worldY, down } — canvas and world coordinates input.stick analog left stick [x, y] Codes are KeyboardEvent.code names ( KeyA , Space , ArrowLeft , ShiftLeft …), Mouse0/1/2 , Gamepad0…n (button index) and GamepadLeft/Right/Up/Down (left stick past 50 %). Gamepads are polled each frame (first connected pad; dead-zone 0.2). Rebinding # input.actions is a plain object — change it at runtime: this.input.actions.jump = [&#39;KeyZ&#39;] and persist it with this.save.set(&#39;bindings&#39;, this.input.actions) . Build a settings screen with game.ui (the library&#39;s ui-settings.js covers volume). Touch # Pointer events cover touch ( input.pointer ). Add a virtual joystick and on-screen buttons ( input.setVirtual(&#39;jump&#39;, true) while held). Tip Use fixedUpdate for physics-related input (jumping) and update for everything else; pressed is evaluated once per rendered frame, so read it in update if you must not miss a tap."},{"f":"assets.html","t":"Assets","s":"Manual","x":"Everything lives under assets/ . Drag files from Finder into the Assets panel to import them (they are copied into the project). Paths in scenes are project-relative strings, e.g. assets/sprites/hero.png . Type Formats Notes Images PNG (also JPG/WebP) spritesheet info in a .meta.json sidecar Audio WAV, OGG, MP3 prefer OGG/WAV for loops; Rust tier: WAV/OGG only Models glTF / GLB animations become named clips Maps Tiled JSON, Nord simple maps Data JSON load with game.loader.json(path) Fonts TTF/OTF/woff2 UI fonts ship in vendor/fonts Sidecar metadata # Only when non-default: assets/foo.png.meta.json Thumbnails # nord thumbs (and the editor, lazily) write previews to .nord-cache/thumbs : real images, first sprite frame with a badge, audio waveforms, models rendered in the 3D view. Built-in placeholder art # The engine ships generated placeholder textures, sprites, UI, audio and skyboxes — see ASSETS.md in the repository for the inventory and how to replace each group. Replace a file at the same path and format and nothing else needs to change. Optional packs # Real CC0 art lives in assets/packs/ . Currently: Kenney 3D Platformer Kit (glTF character/coin/platform models, six OGG sound effects, sprites). Re-fetch with tools/fetch-packs.sh . Copy what you need into your project&#39;s assets/ . Rules # Only use assets you may redistribute. Record packs&#39; licences in THIRD_PARTY.md . Never reference remote URLs — builds fail the offline check ( nord check-offline ). Keep texture sizes sensible (see Performance )."},{"f":"library.html","t":"The library","s":"Manual","x":"The library/ folder holds ~125 tested building blocks, indexed by library/INDEX.json and browsable in the editor&#39;s Library tab or the catalog . Kind Where Example Scripts library/scripts/*.js platformer2d , fps3d , health , ai-chase , wave-spawner , ui-hud Prefabs library/prefabs/ marker Shaders library/shaders/*.glsl crt, dissolve, toon, water, hologram, outline… Snippets library/snippets/ small code recipes ( tween-fade.js ) App kits library/apps/ _kit.js + calculator, todo, kanban, notes, paint… Templates templates/ 17 starter projects For dummies The library is a box of ready-made LEGO bricks. Instead of writing how to make a character jump you drop in platformer2d.js , then change its numbers in the Inspector. Using a library script # Library tab → search → Insert (copies the file into scripts/ , attaches it to the selected entity). Tweak its attributes in the Inspector. Read the file — they are short and meant to be modified. Many scripts talk through events ( events.emit(&#39;score&#39;, 25) , entity.emit(&#39;damage&#39;, {...}) ), so they compose without importing each other. The Platformer example is built almost entirely from library scripts plus two tiny custom ones. Writing your own entry # A library script starts with a header comment that the index builder reads: Run node tools/build-library-index.mjs to regenerate library/INDEX.json (the editor&#39;s Library tab and the catalog read it). Put // Ported from repo / path ( licence ) in the header when you adapt outside code, and list the source in THIRD_PARTY.md . Templates # nord new my-game -t id or the hub. Templates are generated from tools/examples/ id / by node tools/gen_examples.mjs — edit the generator sources, not the output."},{"f":"rust-wasm.html","t":"Rust / WASM","s":"Manual","x":"Two Rust project types sit beside the JS tiers. Both run natively ( cargo run ) and in the browser as WebAssembly. Design record: ADR-019. wasm-game — macroquad + rapier2d; uses the same scene JSON, prefabs and assets as the JS 2D tier. wasm-app — eframe/egui for tools and dashboards. For dummies JavaScript is read and run by the browser on the fly. Rust is translated ahead of time into a compact binary (WebAssembly) that the browser runs much faster. You pay with a build step; you gain speed and Rust&#39;s safety. Layout # Needs rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown . wasm-app also needs the wasm-bindgen CLI at the same version as the crate in Cargo.lock . Scripts # Scenes refer to scripts by registered name. Hooks: start, update, fixed_update (60 Hz), on_destroy, on_collision_enter/exit, on_trigger_enter/exit, on_event(name, value), ui(egui) . Ctx offers position/velocity/impulse, grounded, find/find_tag, spawn_prefab, emit, play (sound), set_text/set_tint/play_anim/flip_x, overlap_box, camera helpers, input() , rand() , restart() . The full list is in docs/RUST_TIER.md in the repository. Building and exporting # What Command dev build nord build-rust [dir] [--release] or the editor&#39;s Build Rust button web export nord export dir --target web-wasm-game (or web-wasm-app ) [--optimize] [--zip] native cargo run in the project In the editor, Play builds first and streams cargo output to the Console with clickable file:line links. Supported components (2D) # sprite, animatedSprite, text, tilemap (+ collider runs), parallax, particles (CPU), camera, rigidbody, collider, trigger, ui label/bar/button/panel (egui), audioSource (autoplay). Not yet: lights, 3D, spawner/path components, joints, animation events. Units: pixels, y down, degrees; physics runs in metres at 32 px/m. Gotchas # Audio is WAV/OGG only. macroquad&#39;s JS bundle logs a harmless register_plugin is not defined error. The page must serve nord.project.json , scenes and assets next to index.html (the export does). Tip Desktop apps in Rust: nord_app_wasm::desktop::Desktop gives files, dialogs, notifications, clipboard, storage with the same permission manifest as JS apps (web builds fall back gracefully). See Desktop apps ."},{"f":"export.html","t":"Export & hosting","s":"Manual","x":"nord build project (alias nord export ) writes a static folder — default project /dist — that needs nothing from the internet. For dummies Exporting is exhaling your project into one plain folder of files. Any web host, USB stick or python3 -m http.server can serve that folder, and anyone with a browser can play it. Targets # Target Output Runtime shipped html folder none (your HTML/JS) web-2d folder nord.js + Phaser web-3d folder nord.js + Babylon packages used + Havok wasm web-wasm-game folder game.wasm + macroquad bundle web-wasm-app folder eframe wasm + glue mac app Name.app (+ .dmg ) NordPlayer shell wrapping any of the above -t auto (default) picks from the project type . nord doctor recommends one. Common options # In the editor: Project → Export… shows the same options and a size report. Output layout # nord.json lists every file with size and sha256. All paths are relative, so sub-folder hosting works. Hosting # Where How Local cd dist python3 -m http.server 8000 Double-click build with --single-file (folder builds need a server: browsers block modules/ fetch on file:// ) itch.io --zip , upload as HTML, tick played in the browser , set viewport to the game size GitHub Pages commit dist/ to gh-pages (or /docs ) and enable Pages nginx root /srv/game; and serve .wasm as application/wasm (default mime types do) Netlify / Cloudflare Pages / S3 upload the folder Full notes: docs/HOSTING.md . Mac app # Wraps the web build in NordPlayer (a wry+tao window shell) and generates the icon set; ad-hoc code signing happens on macOS. Window size, fullscreen and persistent storage follow the project settings. export.mac.bundleId and export.mac.icon live in nord.project.json . The offline guarantee # Every build is scanned for external http(s):// references and fails if your own files contain one ( nord check-offline dir ). Allow a host on purpose with a .offline-allow file. Careful Not done yet: texture-atlas packing and lossy PNG quantisation (task M4-11). The macOS shell and CI bundle step are verified on macOS CI only — see docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md ."},{"f":"performance.html","t":"Performance & debugging","s":"Manual","x":"For dummies Games feel smooth when every frame finishes in ~16 ms (60 fps). Most slowness comes from doing too much every frame — too many objects, too big pictures, creating and throwing away things constantly. Rules of thumb # Reuse, don&#39;t recreate. Use Pool ( new nord.Pool(factory, reset) ) or the library&#39;s util-pool.js for bullets, particles and damage numbers; spawn is async and does JSON work. Textures: keep power-of-two-ish sizes, pack small sprites into one sheet, avoid 4K backgrounds in 2D. nord build --optimize recompresses PNGs losslessly. Physics: use static for anything that never moves; prefer arcade over matter unless you need rotation/polygons; keep triggers small in number. Scripts: avoid allocating arrays in update ; cache find() results in start() ; use fixedUpdate only for physics-coupled code. 3D: one shadow-casting light, shadow map 1024, share materials/models, limit postProcess effects, keep particles capped ( capacity ). DOM UI: update labels only when the value changes. Measuring # util-debug.js (library) shows FPS/entity counts. Browser dev tools: when running with nord serve , open the URL in Chrome/Safari for the Performance tab. game.time.delta gives the last frame time; the scheduler clamps big spikes to 0.25 s so a hiccup can&#39;t explode the simulation. nord build prints a size report; keep an eye on audio — music is usually the biggest asset. Timing facts # The scene runs fixedUpdate at a fixed 60 Hz with at most 5 catch-up steps per frame, then update , component updates and lateUpdate once per frame. Tweens/timers use the scaled dt . Debugging checklist # Red overlay or Console error? Click the file:line . nord check — missing files, duplicate ids, bad scripts. Blank canvas on export? Serve over http (not file:// ) unless you used --single-file . Audio silent? It needs a first click/key — queued sounds then play."},{"f":"reference.html","t":"Reference overview","s":"Manual","x":"Everything below is generated from the source or kept next to it, so it matches the code. I want to know… Go to What this.entity , this.game , this.audio … are game.* namespaces A class&#39;s methods (Entity, Scene, Game, Input, Events, Save, Audio, UI, Scheduler, Rng, Pool, physics adapters) the API section in the sidebar Math/easing helpers Math helpers A component&#39;s fields Component index nord commands Command line JSON file formats Project format Ready-made scripts Library catalog Starter templates and examples Templates examples Editor keys Shortcuts The one import # nord maps to runtime/core/index.js . Backends add components and game.camera / game.physics / game.gui ; scripts never import Phaser or Babylon. Escape hatch # When Nord lacks a wrapper, entity.native (Phaser Container / Babylon TransformNode) and fields like component.obj , .mesh , .light , .camera , .ps , game.camera.native give raw access. Treat it as a last resort: raw objects differ between 2D and 3D and between engine versions."},{"f":"desktop-apps.html","t":"Desktop apps","s":"Manual","x":"A Nord project can be a desktop app , not just a game. Add a desktop block to nord.project.json , call window.nord.desktop.* from your JS (or nord_app_wasm::desktop::Desktop from Rust) and run it in the native player. Everything goes through one Rust backend, nord-desktop , which checks every call against your declared permissions . For dummies Think of it as a phone-app permission list. Your app can only touch what you wrote in desktop.permissions . Nothing declared = it can only keep its own settings and files in its own folder and show dialogs. Quick start # Develop with nord serve . (same API, same permission checks over HTTP), run natively with nord export + nord-player folder , ship with nord bundle-mac / nord bundle-portable ( Packaging ). Three transports, one API # Where Transport What works Native player ( nord-player ) ipc (wry) → Rust everything below nord serve / editor Play http → the same Rust bridge everything (dialogs are native only if the dev machine has the native build; otherwise stubs) Plain browser export web fallbacks store (localStorage), virtual app-data files, Notification , clipboard write, confirm / alert , file picker; the rest rejects with unsupported Permissions ( desktop.permissions ) # Default = least privilege. Review/edit them in the editor: Project ▸ Desktop app permissions… . Files: paths are appData:x (or a bare relative path), documents: , downloads: , temp: (if granted) or an absolute path the user picked in a dialog ( picked ). .. , symlink escapes, drive letters, CON / NUL , alternate streams are all denied; roots can&#39;t be deleted. Prompts: sensitive things (network host, running programs, clipboard read, global shortcuts, local servers, autostart) ask the user once ( prompt: true ), remembered in grants.json . Every call is written to audit.log in the app data folder. ⚠ This is an application-level guard , not an OS sandbox. It limits what the page/scripts can ask for; it does not contain a malicious native binary. API cheat sheet ( window.nord.desktop ) # Area Calls app info() paths() permissions() launch() audit() quit() setQuitGuard() setAutostart() fs readText writeText append read write readJson writeJson list stat exists mkdir remove rename copy watch dialog open save folder recent message confirm notify notify(title, body) , .setBadge(text) , .setProgress(0..1) clipboard readText writeText readImage writeImage store / secret get set remove keys clear · OS-keychain get set delete shell openUrl openPath reveal (http(s)/mailto only; paths must be in scope) process / http run(program, args) (allow-list, no shell) · fetch(url) (host allow-list) window / menu / tray / shortcut open close setTitle setFullscreen setAlwaysOnTop · set context · set remove · register unregister server start({root, port, cors, spa, listing, liveReload, lan, cache}) stop list logs events `on(&#39;menu&#39; Full types: runtime/desktop/nord-desktop.d.ts . Helpers for real-app UX (undo stack, i18n/plurals/Intl formats, dark-mode follow, virtualised lists, pointer-based drag-reorder, roving keyboard focus): library/apps/_ux.js . Rust apps # Native builds call the same backend (same permission checks); web builds fall back as in the table above and return Error { code: unsupported , … } for the rest. Template: Rust desktop app: notes . Example apps (templates in the New-project dialog) # Code editor (CodeMirror: tabs, file tree, highlighting, find/replace, multi-cursor, quick open), local web server (live reload, SPA fallback, CORS, directory listing, request log), notes, file organiser, clipboard manager (tray + global shortcut), markdown editor (live preview). Tip Not verified on a real Mac/Windows yet: native menus/tray/global shortcuts, dock badge. The Linux build (webkit2gtk) runs the full IPC test in CI. See docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md ."},{"f":"_project-format-extra.html","t":"_project-format-extra.md","s":"Manual","x":"Attribute types # Used by script static attributes and component schemas (the inspector draws a widget for each). Type JSON value Options number float min , max , step , default , label int integer min , max bool true/false string text color #rrggbb (3–8 hex digits accepted) vec2 / vec3 [x, y] / [x, y, z] enum one of options options: [...] (script attributes) entity entity name or id asset project-relative path assetType : image audio model json prefab curve list of [t, value] points array list of values element type Invalid values fall back to the default with a console warning; numbers are clamped to min / max ; unknown attribute names warn."},{"f":"tut-t01-hello-nord.html","t":"T01 Hello Nord","s":"Tutorials","x":"You will: create a 2D project, put text on screen, run it. Runnable project: nord new hello -t 2d . For dummies A project is a folder. A scene is one screen/level inside it. Anything you see in the scene is an entity . Today you make one entity that shows words. Create the project: hub → New → 2D game (or nord new hello -t 2d ). Open scenes/main.scene.json in the Hierarchy . Click the Add entity (+) button, name it Title , and set position to [320, 180, 0] (the middle of a 640×360 screen). In the Inspector press + Add component → text and set text = Hello Nord , size = 48 , color = #88c0d0 . Press ▶ Play (Cmd+R). You see the words on the project&#39;s background colour. Change the text while the game runs — the Game tab updates when you save the scene. The scene file you just edited looks like this (you can also paste it in by hand): Next: T02 Sprite movement ."},{"f":"tut-t02-sprite-movement.html","t":"T02 Sprite & movement","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: nord new walker -t topdown-adventure (look at examples/topdown for the finished version). Drag assets/sprites/hero.png from the Assets panel into the viewport: an entity with a sprite appears. Name it Player , add the tag player . Open nord.project.json → input.actions and make sure left , right , up , down exist (templates already have them). Create scripts/walk.js : Select Player , + Add script → walk.js . Play, then change speed in the Inspector. Multiplying by dt makes movement frame-rate independent. input.vec returns a normalised vector, so diagonals aren&#39;t faster. For dummies dt is the time one picture took to draw. Slow computer: big dt, small steps more often skipped; fast computer: small dt. Speed × dt = distance this frame, always the same overall speed. Next: T03 Your first script ."},{"f":"tut-t03-first-script.html","t":"T03 Your first script","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: any 2D project. Library reference: spin.js . Create scripts/pulse.js : Attach it to a sprite. While the game runs , edit amount to 0.5 in the file and save: the object keeps pulsing — hot reload swapped the code without restarting. Add a message: in start() write this.events.emit(&#39;hello&#39;, this.entity.name) and listen in another script with this.events.on(&#39;hello&#39;, (n) = console.log(&#39;hi&#39;, n)) . Look at the Console tab. Break it on purpose (remove a bracket): the game keeps its last good code and the error shows with file:line . Concepts: Scripting . Next: T04 Platformer from template ."},{"f":"tut-t04-platformer.html","t":"T04 Platformer from template","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: nord new jumper -t platformer · finished game: examples/platformer . Play it: A/D run, Space jump, Shift dash, stomp slimes. Select Player . Its scripts are library scripts: platformer2d (movement), health , hero-fx . Change jumpForce 420 → 520, enable wallSlide , enable dash — no code. Open scenes/main.scene.json in the Hierarchy and look at Level (a tilemap ) and the parallax backgrounds. Duplicate a slime (Cmd+D) and move it. Enemies use enemy.js (stomp-or-hurt) plus ai-patrol (library). Add a checkpoint: Library tab → search checkpoint → Insert on a flag entity. How it fits together: scripts talk by events — enemy.js emits score , the score script listens, ui-hud shows it. Read The library . Next: T05 Physics collisions ."},{"f":"tut-t05-physics.html","t":"T05 Physics & collisions","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: the platformer from T04 (finished: examples/platformer ; also see examples/breakout , examples/marble ). A floor: entity with collider (box) and no rigidbody = static. A crate: rigidbody ( dynamic ) + collider . A coin: add a trigger and a script: Make it bounce the player: library physics-bounce-pad.js on a collider entity. Query the world: this.physics.overlapBox(x, y, 40, 40) returns entities in a box — use it for melee hits. Gravity lives in nord.project.json → physics.gravity . Reference: Physics , rigidbody . Next: T06 Tilemaps ."},{"f":"tut-t06-tilemaps.html","t":"T06 Tilemaps","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: examples/platformer (copy via the hub) — uses assets/sprites/tileset_platformer.png (16×16 tiles, 8 columns × 4 rows). Open the Animation / Tilemap panel and switch its mode to Tilemap painter ; choose a *.tilemap.json (or Tiled .tmj ) map. Pick a tile from the palette and paint; right-click erases. Solid tiles are the indices in collides (component or map file). Players and enemies with bodies now stand on them. Read tiles from code: const tm = level.getComponent(&#39;tilemap&#39;); tm.tileAt(x, y) ; change them with tm.setTile(tx, ty, index) (breakable blocks!). Autotiling and dungeon generation: library proc-autotile.js , proc-dungeon.js . Reference: tilemap . Next: T07 Animation juice ."},{"f":"tut-t07-animation-juice.html","t":"T07 Animation & juice","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: examples/platformer ( hero-fx.js , fx-hitstop.js ). Define animations in assets/sprites/hero.png.meta.json ( idle , run , jump ). Switch them from a script: Squash on landing: this.tween(this.entity.scale, [1.3, 0.7, 1], { duration: 0.08, yoyo: true }) . Dust and sparks: add particles and call burst(8) on landing. Impact: this.game.camera.shake(120, 0.008) and the library fx-hitstop.js ( events.emit(&#39;hitstop&#39;, 40) ). Next: T08 UI HUD ."},{"f":"tut-t08-ui-hud.html","t":"T08 UI & HUD","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: examples/space-shooter or examples/platformer . Add the library ui-hud.js to any entity: hearts bound to the Player &#39;s health events, plus a coin counter fed by pickup events. Score: add score.js — it listens to events.emit(&#39;score&#39;, n) , shows a combo-multiplied score and stores the high score in save . Your own label: const l = this.ui.label(&#39;Wave 1&#39;, { anchor: &#39;tr&#39;, x: 12, y: 8, size: 24 }); l.set(&#39;Wave 2&#39;); Menu: ui-main-menu.js and ui-pause.js (library) build screens with ui.menu ; ui-gameover.js shows when the player dies. Make it yours: this.ui.setTheme({ theme: &#39;dracula&#39;, skin: &#39;cyber&#39; }) or set ui.skin / ui.theme in the project file. Reference: UI HUD . Next: T09 Audio ."},{"f":"tut-t09-audio.html","t":"T09 Audio","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: any game ( examples/platformer uses util-sfx.js ). Add the library util-sfx.js to any entity. Now any script can play a sound with this.events.emit(&#39;sfx&#39;, &#39;jump&#39;) . Its map attribute connects events to sounds: { pickup : coin , hit : hit } . Music: this.audio.music(&#39;assets/audio/music/music_adventure.mp3&#39;, { fade: 1.5 }) in a scene script; swap to music_boss.mp3 when the boss appears — it crossfades. Volume UI: ui-settings.js offers master/music/sfx sliders; values persist. Remember browsers wait for a click before playing — your calls are queued, not lost. Reference: Audio . Next: T10 3D scene camera ."},{"f":"tut-t10-3d-scene.html","t":"T10 3D scene & camera","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: nord new box3d -t 3d · look at examples/playground-3d . Ground: entity with mesh shape: ground , size: [20, 0, 20] and a material color: #5e81ac . A cube at [0, 0.5, 0] ( mesh box, castShadow: true ). Sun: entity with light kind: directional , castShadows: true , rotate it [50, -30, 0] . Sky: entity with skybox path: assets/skybox/day . Camera: entity with camera type: orbit , radius: 8 , follow: cube name , userControl: true . Polish: postProcess bloom: true , vignette: 0.3 , tonemap: true . Models: drop assets/packs/kenney-platformer-3d/models/character.glb into your assets/ and add a model . Reference: 3D games . Next: T11 3D physics (FPS) ."},{"f":"tut-t11-3d-physics.html","t":"T11 3D physics (FPS)","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: nord new arena -t fps-arena · finished: examples/fps-arena . The player is an entity with rigidbody (dynamic, fixedRotation ), collider capsule and the library fps3d.js script; the camera is a child called Camera at local y = 0.7 . Tune feel in the Inspector: speed , sprintFactor , jumpSpeed , sensitivity . Crates: rigidbody dynamic + collider box + physics-crate.js (breakable). Walls and floor: collider only. Shooting: physics-hitscan.js casts game.physics.raycast(from, to, { ignore }) from the camera and emits damage on the hit entity. Targets use health and a tiny script that counts kills ( arena.js in the example). Reference: Physics . Next: T12 Enemy AI ."},{"f":"tut-t12-enemy-ai.html","t":"T12 Enemy AI","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: examples/topdown (bats), examples/tower-defense (waves). Patrol: ai-patrol.js walks between two points; set distance and speed . Chase: ai-chase.js — attributes target (a tag , default player ), sight , lose , speed , keepDistance . The enemy chases when the target comes within sight and gives up beyond lose . Combine states with ai-fsm.js , or build trees with ai-behaviour-tree.js . Damage: contact scripts emit entity.emit(&#39;damage&#39;, { amount: 1, from }) ; health.js handles hit points and invulnerability frames. Waves: wave-spawner.js ( prefabs , base , growth , waves ) emits wave:start , wave:clear , wave:all-done . Pathfinding on grids: ai-astar.js . Next: T13 Save load ."},{"f":"tut-t13-save-load.html","t":"T13 Save & load","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: examples/idle-clicker (autosave + offline earnings). Try it here (a throw-away in-memory store): Data is JSON in localStorage under nord: project id :slot n , with a version ; pass migrate(data, fromVersion) to new Save when your format changes. Library save-slots.js : tag entities persist , then events.emit(&#39;game:save&#39;, 1) / &#39;game:load&#39; . Rust tier: use the platform&#39;s storage via egui/macroquad APIs (not covered by Ctx yet). Next: T14 Export host ."},{"f":"tut-t14-export-host.html","t":"T14 Export to web & host it","s":"Tutorials","x":"nord check my-game — validates the project. nord build my-game --minify --optimize --zip (or Project → Export… ). Test locally: cd my-game/dist python3 -m http.server 8000 , open http://localhost:8000 . Offline proof: nord check-offline my-game/dist must pass (the build already runs it). Upload: itch.io (zip, played in the browser ), GitHub Pages ( dist/ to gh-pages ), Netlify/Cloudflare Pages (drag the folder). For a file you can double-click: nord build my-game --single-file . Details: Export hosting . Next: T15 Mac app ."},{"f":"tut-t15-mac-app.html","t":"T15 Mac app","s":"Tutorials","x":"Requires macOS and the prebuilt player ( nord-player , built in CI or with cargo build -p nord-player --features shell ). Set export.mac.bundleId and an icon in nord.project.json (a 1024 px PNG). nord bundle-mac my-game --name My Game --icon icon.png --dmg -o ~/Desktop . Open the .app . The window size, fullscreen (Cmd+Ctrl+F) and persistent storage come from the project/player. The bundle is ad-hoc signed; for distribution to other Macs you will need your own Developer ID signing and notarisation. Careful The macOS shell has been verified only through CI so far (see Known issues ); tell us if the webview misbehaves. Next: T16 Rust/WASM game ."},{"f":"tut-t16-rust-game.html","t":"T16 Rust/WASM game","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: nord new crab -t rust-2d-game · finished: examples/rust-platformer . rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown . Open src/main.rs : scripts are structs implementing Script , registered by name. The scene JSON refers to them ( scripts : [{ path : player }] ). Press ▶ Play in the editor (it builds first) or nord build-rust . nord serve . . Natively: cargo run . Edit speed in Registry::register( player , |a| … a.f32( speed , 130.0) …) , rebuild, play. Export: nord export . --target web-wasm-game --optimize --zip . Reference: Rust / WASM . Next: T17 Rust egui app ."},{"f":"tut-t17-rust-egui-app.html","t":"T17 Rust egui app","s":"Tutorials","x":"Project: nord new tool -t rust-egui-app · finished: examples/rust-json-tool (JSON editor with tree, formatter, drag-and-drop). The app type is wasm-app ; src/main.rs implements eframe::App and calls nord_app_wasm::launch . Native: cargo run . Web: nord build-rust . nord serve . . Export: nord export . --target web-wasm-app — needs the wasm-bindgen CLI at the version in Cargo.lock . For plain-DOM apps without Rust, use the JS app kit instead ( library/apps/_kit.js , templates ui-app ). Tip Native file dialogs, notifications and storage: use nord_app_wasm::desktop::Desktop — see Desktop apps and the template Rust desktop app: notes . Next: T18 Writing library snippets ."},{"f":"tut-t18-library-snippets.html","t":"T18 Writing library snippets","s":"Tutorials","x":"Write the script in library/scripts/fx-flash.js : Rules of thumb: small, event-driven ( entity.on / events.on ), clean up in onDestroy , expose tweakable numbers as attributes, work for 2D and 3D when feasible. Run node tools/build-library-index.mjs — the script appears in the editor&#39;s Library tab and the catalog . Test it in an example ( node tools/smoke-examples.mjs id ) and add a line to docs/CHANGELOG.md . That&#39;s the end of the tutorial track — see the Cookbook for more recipes."},{"f":"cookbook.html","t":"Cookbook","s":"Guides","x":"Each recipe is plain Nord API — no Phaser/Babylon calls. Movement feel # Coyote time + jump buffer — use platformer2d.js ( coyoteTime , jumpBuffer ), or in your own script: remember this.lastGrounded = time.elapsed while grounded and allow a jump if elapsed - lastGrounded 0.1 . Smooth camera — camera component with follow and lerp: 0.1 ; add deadzone: [60, 40] . Screen shake on hit Slow motion Spawning # Bullets with a lifetime Random spawn points — this.rng.pick(this.scene.findByTag(&#39;spawn&#39;)).worldPos . rng is seedable ( new Rng(seed) ) for replays. Timers tweens # Data # High score : if (score this.save.get(&#39;best&#39;, 0)) this.save.set(&#39;best&#39;, score); Settings screen : this.audio.volume(&#39;music&#39;, v) is persisted automatically. UI # Floating damage numbers : library ui-damage-numbers.js , triggered by events.emit(&#39;dmg&#39;, { pos, amount }) . Tooltip / dialogue : dialogue.js reads assets/story.json nodes ( speaker , text , next / choices ). 3D # Third-person follow — third-person3d.js ; orbit — camera type: orbit + follow . Ground check — this.physics.isGrounded(this.entity) . Interact with what you look at — interact-ray.js casts from the camera. Debug # this.game.timeScale , this.game.pause() , this.events.on(&#39;error&#39;, e = …) , util-debug.js ."},{"f":"cheat-sheet-nordjs.html","t":"Cheat sheet — nord.js","s":"Guides","x":"Need Code Attribute this.attrs.speed Move this.entity.pos = [x, y, 0] or edit then this.entity.touch() Find this.scene.find(&#39;Name&#39;) , findByTag(&#39;t&#39;) , entity.find(&#39;Child&#39;) Component entity.getComponent(&#39;sprite&#39;) , entity.set(&#39;sprite&#39;,&#39;tint&#39;,&#39;#f00&#39;) Other script entity.getScript(OtherClass) Spawn await this.scene.spawn(&#39;prefabs/x.prefab.json&#39;, { pos: [x,y,0] }) Destroy entity.destroy() Scene this.game.loadScene(&#39;scenes/l2.scene.json&#39;) , this.game.restart() Input input.down/pressed/released(&#39;jump&#39;) , axis(&#39;left&#39;,&#39;right&#39;) , vec(l,r,u,d) , pointer Events this.events.on(&#39;score&#39;, fn) → returns off; emit(&#39;score&#39;, 5) ; per entity entity.on/emit Timers this.after(s, fn) , this.every(s, fn) , this.tween(obj, to, {duration, ease, yoyo, repeat}) Audio audio.play(path, {volume, loop}) , audio.music(path, {fade}) , audio.volume(&#39;music&#39;, .5) UI ui.label/button/bar/panel/image/menu , ui.show/hide(&#39;screen&#39;) Save save.get(k, def) , save.set(k, v) , save.slot(2) Physics physics.setVelocity(e, vx, vy) , applyImpulse , isGrounded(e) , overlapBox (2D), raycast (3D) Camera (2D) game.camera.shake/flash/fade/zoom/follow/center Random this.rng.range(a,b) , int , pick(arr) , chance(p) , shuffle(arr) Pause/slow game.pause() , game.resume() , game.timeScale = 0.5 Details: API reference ."},{"f":"cheat-sheet-export.html","t":"Cheat sheet — export","s":"Guides","x":"Goal Command Check project nord check . Dev server (live reload) nord serve . -p 4173 Web build nord build . → dist/ Smaller nord build . --minify --optimize itch.io zip nord build . --zip Double-clickable nord build . --single-file Rust game / app nord export . --target web-wasm-game / web-wasm-app Dev build of Rust nord build-rust . Mac app nord bundle-mac . --name My Game --icon icon.png --dmg Offline scan nord check-offline dist Toolchain advice nord doctor Details: Export hosting , CLI ."},{"f":"glossary.html","t":"Glossary","s":"Guides","x":"For dummies Keep this open in a tab while you read — each term has a one-line kitchen version. Term Meaning Entity A thing in your scene (a player, a coin, a light). An empty box until it has components. Component A piece of data/behaviour on an entity: what it looks like , how it collides . Script JavaScript (or Rust) attached to an entity that decides what it does . Attribute A number/option on a script that you tweak in the Inspector instead of the code. Scene One level/screen: a JSON file listing entities. Prefab A saved entity you can stamp many times (bullet, enemy). Tag / Layer Labels to find groups of entities / to order them. Transform Position, rotation, scale. Frame / dt One drawn picture / the seconds it took. Multiply speeds by dt. Fixed update Code that runs at exactly 60 times per second, used with physics. Rigidbody / Collider Obeys physics / this is my shape . Trigger An invisible zone that notices when something enters it. Tilemap / Tileset A level built from small repeating pictures on a grid / the picture sheet. Spritesheet One image containing many animation frames. Bus (audio) A volume group: music, sfx, ui, master. Hot reload Changing code while the game is running, without restarting. Tier Which engine runs the project: HTML, 2D, 3D, Rust game, Rust app. Export Packing the game into a folder others can play. WASM / WebAssembly A fast compiled format browsers can run (used for Rust and 3D physics). Escape hatch entity.native : direct access to the raw Phaser/Babylon object."},{"f":"shortcuts.html","t":"Keyboard shortcuts","s":"Guides","x":"Editor shortcuts on macOS (Ctrl replaces Cmd on other platforms). Also listed in Settings (Cmd+,) . Keys Action Cmd+P Quick open file Cmd+K Command palette Cmd+S Save Cmd+Z / Shift+Cmd+Z Undo / redo Cmd+C / Cmd+V / Cmd+D Copy / paste / duplicate entity Delete Delete selection W / E / R Move / rotate / scale gizmo F Focus selection in viewport Cmd+R / Cmd+. Play (toggles stop) / stop — rebind above Cmd+F / Cmd+Opt+F Find / replace in file Cmd+Shift+F Search in project Cmd+G Go to line Cmd+/ Toggle comment Tab / Shift+Tab Indent / dedent Ctrl+Space Autocomplete Cmd+1/2/3/4 Show Scene / Game / Code / Assets Cmd+, Settings"},{"f":"api-namespaces.html","t":"game.* namespaces","s":"API","x":"Everything reachable from a script. Nord namespaces are the same for Phaser (2D) and Babylon (3D) unless marked. this (inside a NordScript) Every script gets these. They are shortcuts to the objects below. this.entity The entity the script is attached to. this.scene The running scene (spawn, find). this.game The game: loadScene, restart, pause, camera, physics, ui… this.attrs Resolved attribute values (defaults merged with scene overrides, clamped). this.input Action-based input. this.audio Sounds and music. this.time { delta, elapsed } in seconds (scaled by timeScale). this.events Global event bus. this.ui DOM HUD/menu layer. this.physics Physics adapter for the project's engine. this.save Save slots. this.rng Seedable random numbers. this.tween(target, to, opts) Animate numeric properties of an object or array. opts: duration, delay, ease, yoyo, repeat, onUpdate, onComplete. Returns cancel(). this.after(sec, fn) Run once later. Returns cancel(). this.every(sec, fn) Run repeatedly. Returns cancel(). game The Game object (`this.game`). game.loadScene(path) Load a scene file (default: project startScene). Destroys the current scene. game.restart() Reload the current scene. game.pause() / resume() Pause the update loop (emits 'pause'/'resume'). game.timeScale Number; 0.5 = slow motion. Applies to dt, timers and tweens. game.paused Boolean. game.project Parsed nord.project.json. game.events Global bus. Built-in events: sceneLoad, sceneUnload, pause, resume, error, hotReload. game.preload(paths) Load textures/models ahead of time. game.errors Errors caught from scripts so far. game.camera Camera effects. camera.shake(ms=250, intensity=0.01) 2D Screen shake. camera.flash(ms=250, color='#ffffff') 2D Flash overlay. camera.fade(ms=500, color='#000000') 2D Fade out to a colour. camera.fadeIn(ms=500) 2D Fade back in. camera.zoom(z, ms=0) 2D Set or tween the zoom. camera.follow(entity, lerp) 2D Follow an entity (smoothing 0..1). camera.center(x, y) 2D Jump to a point. camera.native 2D The Phaser camera (escape hatch). 3D camera 3D Use a `camera` component (free/orbit); `getComponent('camera').activate()` switches the current one. Effects: add a `postProcess` component. game.physics Physics adapter. Methods differ slightly by engine; see the adapter pages. physics.setVelocity(entity, vx, vy[, vz]) Set velocity (2D px/s, 3D m/s). 2D: pass null to keep an axis. physics.velocity(entity) Current velocity array. physics.applyImpulse(entity, ix, iy[, iz]) Instant push. physics.setGravity(x, y[, z]) Change world gravity. physics.isGrounded(entity) Standing on something? (2D: touching a body below; 3D: short ray down.) physics.overlapBox(x, y, w, h) 2D Entities overlapping a box (2D) physics.raycast(from, to, {ignore}) 3D First hit {entity, point, normal, distance} or null. physics.teleport(entity, ...pos) Move a body without keeping velocity. game.ui DOM UI layer drawn over the canvas (see the UI chapter). Works in 2D and 3D. ui.label / button / panel / image / bar / menu Create widgets. Each returns a handle with `el`, `remove()` and (label/bar) `set(value)`. ui.show / hide / toggle(screen) Show a named screen (layer). 'hud' is always created and shown. ui.setTheme({skin, theme}) Switch fonts and colours at runtime. ui.theme({accent: '#…'}) Override single colour variables. game.gui Babylon GUI bridge (3D only; null if the GUI library is not loaded). gui.label / button / bar Screen-space controls (Babylon GUI). gui.worldLabel(entity, text) Label that floats above an entity. gui.attach(control, entity) Link any control to an entity. gui.remove(control) Dispose a control. game.audio Audio manager (see the Audio chapter). audio.play(path, {volume, loop, rate, bus, pan}) One-shot or loop. Returns {stop()}. audio.music(path, {fade, volume}) Crossfade to a looping track. audio.stopMusic({fade}) Fade out the music. audio.volume(bus, v) Get/set a bus volume (master, music, sfx, ui). Persisted. audio.mute(bus, on) Mute a bus. audio.preload(paths) Decode ahead of time. game.save Persistent key/value storage in slots (localStorage; versioned). save.get(key, default) Read. save.set(key, value) Write (JSON-serialisable). save.slot(n) Select a slot (chainable). save.has(n) / delete(n) / all() Slot management. entity.native Escape hatch: the underlying engine object. Only reach for it when Nord has no wrapper. entity.native (2D) Phaser Container holding the visuals. entity.native (3D) Babylon TransformNode. component.obj / .mesh / .light / .camera / .ps Raw engine objects on many runtime components."},{"f":"api-nordscript.html","t":"NordScript","s":"API","x":"Base class of every script. Source: runtime/core/script.js NordScript base class + attribute handling. See docs/SCRIPTING_API.md. Properties scene read-only game read-only input read-only audio read-only time read-only events read-only ui read-only physics read-only save read-only rng read-only Methods tween(target, to, opts) after(sec, fn) every(sec, fn)"},{"f":"api-entity.html","t":"Entity","s":"API","x":"A node in the scene tree. Source: runtime/core/entity.js Properties pos read / write transform — setters notify the backend so native objects stay in sync rot read / write scale read / write worldPos read-only World position: parent chain applied with scale + translation only (rotation is applied by the backend natively). enabled read / write Methods touch() Call after mutating pos/rot/scale arrays in place (e.g. `e.pos[0] += 1; e.touch()`). *all() find(name) findByTag(tag) hasTag(tag) getComponent(type) addComponent(type, data = {}) set(type, key, value) Change a component field at runtime (`e.set('sprite','tint','#f00')`); components without a `set` just store the value. getScript(Class) addScript(Class, attrs = {}) on(n, f) off(n, f) emit(n, d) callScripts(method, ...args) Run `fn` on every enabled script (errors are caught + reported so one bad script cannot kill the frame). destroy() clone() toJSON(fresh = false)"},{"f":"api-scene.html","t":"Scene","s":"API","x":"The running scene: entities, lifecycle, prefabs. Source: runtime/core/scene.js Methods *all() find(name) findById(id) findByTag(tag) async build() Build entities (and their components/scripts) from scene data. Scripts are resolved via game.loadScript(path). async spawnData(data, parent = null) Spawn at runtime from entity JSON (ids regenerated). Lifecycle runs on the next frame boundary. async spawn(path, { pos, rot, scale, overrides, parent = null, name } = {}) Spawn a prefab file: `scene.spawn('prefabs/bullet.prefab.json', { pos: [x,y,0], overrides: {...} })`. start() init() all, then start() all, once the whole tree exists. tick(dt) Advance one frame. `dt` is real seconds; game.timeScale applied here. destroyEntity(e) dispatch(entity, method, ...args) Physics/backends call this: `scene.dispatch(a, 'onCollisionEnter', b, contact)`. destroy()"},{"f":"api-game.html","t":"Game","s":"API","x":"Owns the scene and shared services ( this.game ). Source: runtime/core/game.js Methods warn(m) reportError(err, script, method) loadJSON(path) async loadScript(path, { fresh = false } = {}) async hotReloadScript(path) Re-import a script module (cache-busted) and swap the prototype of every live instance, keeping its fields. Each instance gets `onHotReload(oldState)` if defined (oldState = shallow copy of its own fields). Attributes are re-resolved against the new class so added/removed attributes take effect. Relative imports *of* the script are not re-fetched. Returns the number of instances patched; on a syntax/runtime error nothing changes and the error is shown/returned. hotReload(paths) Dev-server hook (see serve.rs): returns true if every changed file was a script we hot-swapped; false → caller reloads the page. async loadScene(path = this.project.startScene) restart() pause() resume() tick(dt) Backends call this from their render loop with real delta seconds."},{"f":"api-input.html","t":"Input","s":"API","x":"Action-based input ( this.input ). Source: runtime/core/input.js Action-based input. Pure state machine; DOM wiring in `attachDom`. Actions map to codes like 'KeyA', 'Space', 'Mouse0', 'Gamepad0' (button 0), 'GamepadLeft'/'GamepadRight'/'GamepadUp'/'GamepadDown' (left stick). Methods setKey(code, down) setVirtual(action, on) beginFrame() Call once per frame before scripts update: snapshots action state so pressed/released work. down(a) pressed(a) released(a) key(code) axis(neg, pos) vec(l, r, u, d) Normalised 2D vector from four actions (analog stick wins when deflected). pollGamepad(pad) Feed gamepad state (navigator.getGamepads()[i]-shaped). Buttons map to 'Gamepad n ', left stick to Gamepad{Left,Right,Up,Down}."},{"f":"api-events.html","t":"Events","s":"API","x":"Event emitter ( this.events , entity.on ). Source: runtime/core/events.js Tiny event emitter used for the global bus and per-entity events. Methods on(name, fn) once(name, fn) off(name, fn) emit(name, data) clear()"},{"f":"api-save.html","t":"Save","s":"API","x":"Versioned save slots ( this.save ). Source: runtime/core/save.js Versioned save slots on top of localStorage (or any Storage-like object). Methods slot(n) get(k, def) set(k, v) all() has(n = this.slotId) delete(n = this.slotId)"},{"f":"api-audio.html","t":"Audio","s":"API","x":"Buses, sfx, music ( this.audio ). Source: runtime/core/audio.js Properties musicPath read-only Methods attach(target = globalThis.window) Browsers need a user gesture; attach() resumes the context on the first one and replays queued calls. async unlock() async load(path) preload(paths) play(path, { volume = 1, loop = false, rate = 1, bus = 'sfx', pan = 0 } = {}) Play a one-shot (or loop). Returns a handle { stop(), source } or null if audio is unavailable. async music(path, { fade = 1, volume = 1 } = {}) Start looping music, crossfading from the current track. Same path again is a no-op. stopMusic({ fade = 0.5 } = {}) volume(bus, v) mute(bus = 'master', on = true) isMuted(bus = 'master') suspend() Pause/resume the whole context (used when the game is paused or tab hidden). resume()"},{"f":"api-ui.html","t":"UI","s":"API","x":"DOM HUD/menu layer ( this.ui ). Source: runtime/core/ui.js Methods screen(name) show(name) hide(name) toggle(name) isShown(name) setTheme(o) Switch skin/theme at runtime: ui.setTheme({ theme: 'dracula', skin: 'cyber' }). theme(vars) Override individual variables, e.g. ui.theme({ accent: '#ebcb8b' }). panel(o = {}) label(text = '', o = {}) button(text, onClick, o = {}) image(src, o = {}) bar(value = 1, o = {}) Progress bar; bar.set(0..1). menu(screen, items, { x = 0, y = 0, gap = 12 } = {}) Convenience: vertical menu of buttons. items: [{text, onClick}] clear(screen) destroy()"},{"f":"api-scheduler.html","t":"Scheduler","s":"API","x":"Timers and tweens ( this.after/every/tween ). Source: runtime/core/util.js Timers + tweens, advanced by `update(dt)` with the (time-scaled) frame delta. Properties size read-only Methods after(sec, fn) Run fn once after `sec`. Returns cancel(). every(sec, fn) Run fn every `sec` until cancelled. tween(target, to, opts = {}) Tween numeric props of `target` (object or array) to `to`. opts: duration, delay, ease, onUpdate, onComplete, yoyo, repeat update(dt) clear()"},{"f":"api-rng.html","t":"Rng","s":"API","x":"Seedable random numbers ( this.rng ). Source: runtime/core/util.js Seedable rng (mulberry32). Methods next() range(a, b) int(a, b) pick(arr) chance(p) shuffle(arr)"},{"f":"api-pool.html","t":"Pool","s":"API","x":"Object pool. Source: runtime/core/util.js Properties available read-only Methods get() release(o)"},{"f":"api-arcadeadapter.html","t":"ArcadeAdapter","s":"API","x":"2D physics (Arcade) — this.physics in 2D games. Source: runtime/phaser/physics.js · extends Base Methods create(entity) step(dt) body(e) setVelocity(e, vx, vy) velocity(e) applyImpulse(e, ix, iy) isGrounded(e) setGravity(x, y) overlapBox(x, y, w, h) Things overlapping the box centred at (x, y) with size w×h (world px): entities with bodies, plus `{ tile: true }` markers for solid tilemap tiles. enable(e, on) teleport(e, x, y)"},{"f":"api-matteradapter.html","t":"MatterAdapter","s":"API","x":"2D physics (Matter) — this.physics when physics.engine is matter . Source: runtime/phaser/physics.js · extends Base Methods create(entity) step(dt) rec(e) setVelocity(e, vx, vy) velocity(e) applyImpulse(e, ix, iy) isGrounded(e) setGravity(x, y) overlapBox(x, y, w, h) Entities whose bodies overlap the box centred at (x, y) with size w×h (world px). enable(e, on) teleport(e, x, y)"},{"f":"api-havokadapter.html","t":"HavokAdapter","s":"API","x":"3D physics (Havok) — this.physics in 3D games. Source: runtime/babylon/physics.js Methods build(e) flushDirty() rec(e) create(e) forget(e) step() teleported(e, keepVelocity = false) Entity transform was set from script: push it into Havok for the next step (pre-step sync is off by default), zeroing velocity for dynamic bodies. setVelocity(e, x, y, z) velocity(e) applyImpulse(e, ix, iy, iz) setGravity(x, y, z) raycast(from, to, { ignore = null } = {}) Cast a ray; returns { entity, point, normal, distance } or null. `ignore` = entity to skip (e.g. the caster). isGrounded(e, tolerance = 0.12) enable(e, on) teleport(e, x, y, z)"},{"f":"api-helpers.html","t":"Math & helpers","s":"API","x":"Small engine-agnostic helpers exported from nord ( runtime/core/util.js ). Functions clamp(v, a, b) lerp(a, b, t) remap(v, a, b, c, d) deg2rad(d) rad2deg(r) angleDiff(a, b) vec Array-based vectors (2 or 3 components): vec.add, sub, scale, len, dist, norm, lerp . Easing Names for tween({ease}) : linear , quadIn , quadOut , quadInOut , cubicIn , cubicOut , cubicInOut , sineInOut , backOut , elasticOut , bounceOut , bounceIn . A function t→t also works."},{"f":"component-sprite.html","t":"sprite","s":"Components","x":"A single image (or one frame of a spritesheet) drawn at the entity position. 2D type: \"sprite\" For dummies A sprite is just a picture. Give it an image file and the entity shows that picture on screen. Fields Field Type Default texture asset(image) — frame int — tint color — alpha number 1 flipX bool — flipY bool — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes texture is a path like assets/sprites/hero.png . Spritesheets use frame to pick the cell (frame size comes from the .meta.json sidecar, see Assets). tint multiplies the colour (white = unchanged). alpha is 0..1. Extra data keys honoured by the runtime: origin [x,y] (default [0.5,0.5]), size [w,h] to stretch, blend (normal|add|multiply|screen). In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('sprite') . Live-settable with entity.set('sprite', key, value) : texture , frame , tint , flipX , flipY , alpha . Other keys are just stored."},{"f":"component-animatedSprite.html","t":"animatedSprite","s":"Components","x":"A spritesheet with named animations. Animations are declared in the texture's `.meta.json` (`anims`). 2D type: \"animatedSprite\" For dummies Like a flip-book: a strip of little pictures played quickly one after another makes the character walk. Fields Field Type Default texture asset(image) — anim string — autoplay bool true speed number 1 flipX bool — flipY bool — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes anim is the animation that starts playing (unless autoplay is false). speed scales playback. Animation names live in assets/foo.png.meta.json → anims: { walk: { frames: [0,3], fps: 10, loop: true } } . In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('animatedSprite') . play(name, {restart}) — switch animation (no-op if already playing, unless restart) stop() setFlip(x, y=false) current — name of the playing animation Live-settable with entity.set('animatedSprite', key, value) : anim , speed , tint . Other keys are just stored."},{"f":"component-text.html","t":"text","s":"Components","x":"World-space text (Phaser Text). For HUD text prefer `uiLabel`. 2D type: \"text\" For dummies Words that live inside the game world and move with the camera, like a sign. Fields Field Type Default text string — size int 16 color color — font string — align enum(left|center|right) — bold bool — stroke color — strokeThickness int — wrapWidth int — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes Anchored by origin (default centre). wrapWidth enables word wrap. In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('text') . Live-settable with entity.set('text', key, value) : text , color . Other keys are just stored."},{"f":"component-tilemap.html","t":"tilemap","s":"Components","x":"A grid of tiles from a tileset image. Loads Tiled JSON (`.tmj`/`.json`) or Nord simple maps (`layers[].data`). 2D type: \"tilemap\" For dummies Build levels by stamping small square pictures (tiles) on a grid, like LEGO baseplates. Fields Field Type Default map asset(json) — tileset asset(image) — tilesetName string — collides bool — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes collides — tile indices that block bodies. Tilemaps are static and live in world space (their entity transform is read once at creation). Optional data keys: margin , spacing , collideLayer , tilesetName (Tiled). In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('tilemap') . tileAt(worldX, worldY, layer=0) → tile index or -1 setTile(tx, ty, index, layer=0) layers — Phaser tile layers (escape hatch) map — Phaser Tilemap"},{"f":"component-parallax.html","t":"parallax","s":"Components","x":"A screen-filling background layer that scrolls slower than the camera for depth. 2D type: \"parallax\" For dummies Far-away hills move slowly while nearby trees move fast — that is what makes it feel deep. Fields Field Type Default texture asset(image) — factor number 0.5 fit bool true scale number 1 Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes factor is [x, y] scroll speed relative to the camera (0 = fixed, 1 = moves with the world). Defaults: factor 0.5 (x), data key factor:[0.5,0] . fit scales the image to the screen height. Put several parallax entities at different layers. In a scene"},{"f":"component-particles.html","t":"particles","s":"Components","x":"Particle emitter. 2D (Phaser emitter) and 3D (Babylon ParticleSystem) use the same component with tier-specific fields. 2D + 3D type: \"particles\" For dummies Lots of tiny pictures that fly out and fade — sparks, smoke, dust, magic. Fields Field Type Default texture asset(image) — lifespan number 1000 speed number 100 angle number — gravityY number — scale number 1 alpha number 1 tint color — frequency number 50 quantity int 1 burst bool — emitting bool true blend enum(normal|add|multiply|screen) — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes 2D: lifespan ms, speed [min,max] px/s, angle [min,max] degrees, scale [start,end], alpha [start,end], frequency ms between emissions, burst N to fire once. 3D: lifetime [min,max] s, size [min,max], speed [min,max] power, rate /s, capacity , gravity vec3, direction1/2 , emitMin/emitMax , color1/color2 . blend : additive by default; set normal for smoke. In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('particles') . burst(n) — emit n particles now start() stop()"},{"f":"component-camera.html","t":"camera","s":"Components","x":"2D: configures the main camera (zoom, follow, bounds). 3D: creates a free (first-person) or orbit camera. 2D + 3D type: \"camera\" For dummies The camera is the window you look at the game through. It can follow the player. Fields Field Type Default zoom number 1 follow entity — lerp number 0.1 background color — deadzone vec2 — roundPixels bool — fov number 60 near number 0.1 far number 1000 type enum(free|follow|arcRotate) follow active bool true Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes 2D extras: bounds [x,y,w,h], deadzone , lerp (follow smoothing 0..1), roundPixels . Without follow the camera centres on its entity. 3D: type orbit creates an ArcRotateCamera ( alpha , beta , radius , minRadius , maxRadius , follow ); anything else creates a FreeCamera parented to the entity. userControl lets the player look around; active:false creates it without making it current. Use game.camera (2D) for shake/flash/fade/zoom. In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('camera') . follow(entityOrNameOrId, lerp) — 2D and 3D orbit snapTo(x, y) — 2D activate() — 3D, make this the current camera"},{"f":"component-light.html","t":"light","s":"Components","x":"2D: Phaser point light (turns on the light pipeline for all sprites). 3D: point / directional / spot / hemispheric light, optional shadows. 2D + 3D type: \"light\" For dummies A lamp. In 2D things near it get brighter; in 3D it lights models and can cast shadows. Fields Field Type Default kind enum(point|directional|spot|hemispheric) point color color — intensity number 1 radius number 200 range number 10 flicker number — castShadows bool — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes 2D: radius px, intensity , color , flicker 0..1 (random flicker), optional offset [x,y]. 3D: kind , range , castShadows (directional/spot only; one shadow generator per scene), direction vec3 for directional, angle / exponent for spot, groundColor for hemispheric, shadowMapSize . In a scene"},{"f":"component-mesh.html","t":"mesh","s":"Components","x":"A primitive 3D shape (box, sphere, cylinder, capsule, torus, plane, ground). 3D type: \"mesh\" For dummies Basic 3D building blocks — cubes, balls, tubes — good for prototyping before you have real models. Fields Field Type Default shape enum(box|sphere|cylinder|plane|ground|capsule|torus) box size vec3 — segments int 24 castShadow bool true receiveShadow bool true Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes size is [x,y,z] (sphere uses x as diameter; ground uses x and z). Pair with a material component to colour it. In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('mesh') . Live-settable with entity.set('mesh', key, value) : visible . Other keys are just stored."},{"f":"component-model.html","t":"model","s":"Components","x":"A glTF/GLB model instanced into the scene, with its animations. 3D type: \"model\" For dummies A ready-made 3D object (a character, a tree) made in Blender or downloaded as a .glb file. Fields Field Type Default path asset(model) — animation string — speed number 1 shadow bool — unique bool — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes animation starts a clip on load; speed scales it. unique clones the geometry instead of sharing instances. A material component on the same entity overrides the model&#39;s materials. In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('model') . play(name, {loop=true, speed}) — start an animation clip stop() animations — Map of clip name → Babylon AnimationGroup"},{"f":"component-material.html","t":"material","s":"Components","x":"PBR (or unlit) material applied to the meshes of this entity. 3D type: \"material\" For dummies The paint and finish of a 3D object: colour, shininess, a texture on top. Fields Field Type Default color color — metallic number — roughness number 0.8 texture asset(image) — normalMap asset(image) — emissive color — alpha number 1 doubleSided bool — unlit bool — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes metallic / roughness 0..1. texture = albedo, normalMap for bumps, emissive glows. unlit skips lighting (flat colour). Optional uvScale [u,v] to tile textures. In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('material') . Live-settable with entity.set('material', key, value) : color , metallic , roughness , alpha , emissive . Other keys are just stored."},{"f":"component-skybox.html","t":"skybox","s":"Components","x":"Cube-map sky. `path` is a folder containing px/nx/py/ny/pz/nz.png (e.g. `assets/skybox/day`). 3D type: \"skybox\" For dummies A big painted box around the world so you see sky and horizon instead of empty void. Fields Field Type Default path asset(image) — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . In a scene"},{"f":"component-postProcess.html","t":"postProcess","s":"Components","x":"Full-screen effects on the active camera: bloom, FXAA/MSAA, vignette, tone mapping, grain, sharpen, chromatic aberration. 3D type: \"postProcess\" For dummies Instagram filters for your game: glow, dark corners, film grain. Fields Field Type Default bloom bool — bloomThreshold number 0.8 bloomWeight number 0.3 fxaa bool — vignette number — contrast number 1 exposure number 1 tonemap bool — grain number — sharpen number — chromaticAberration number — msaa int — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes Created on the first frame after a camera exists. exposure / contrast tweak the final image. Changing a field at runtime re-applies the pipeline. In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('postProcess') . Live-settable with entity.set('postProcess', key, value) : (any field) . Other keys are just stored."},{"f":"component-rigidbody.html","t":"rigidbody","s":"Components","x":"Makes the entity a physics body. Add a `collider` for its shape. 2D + 3D type: \"rigidbody\" For dummies Tells physics this thing is real : it can fall, bounce and be pushed. Fields Field Type Default mode enum(dynamic|static|kinematic) dynamic mass number 1 drag number 0.1 gravityScale number 1 bounce number — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes mode : dynamic (simulated), static (never moves), kinematic (moved by your velocity, pushes others). 2D engine is chosen in nord.project.json → physics.engine ( arcade default, or matter ); 3D uses Havok. Rotation is locked by default in 2D ( fixedRotation: true ). Extra keys: maxVelocity , fixedRotation , friction . In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('rigidbody') ."},{"f":"component-collider.html","t":"collider","s":"Components","x":"Collision shape. Without an explicit `size` it is taken from the sprite/mesh bounds. 2D + 3D type: \"collider\" For dummies The invisible outline physics uses to know where your object ends. Fields Field Type Default shape enum(box|circle|sphere|capsule) box size vec3 — radius number — offset vec3 — trigger bool — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes 2D shapes: box, circle. 3D: box, sphere, capsule (and cylinder). offset shifts the shape. trigger: true detects overlaps without blocking. In a scene"},{"f":"component-trigger.html","t":"trigger","s":"Components","x":"Shorthand for a collider with `trigger: true` — an invisible zone that fires `onTriggerEnter` / `onTriggerExit`. 2D + 3D type: \"trigger\" For dummies An invisible tripwire: walking into it makes something happen (pick up a coin, open a door). Fields Field Type Default shape enum(box|circle|sphere) box size vec3 — radius number — offset vec3 — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . In a scene"},{"f":"component-audioSource.html","t":"audioSource","s":"Components","x":"Plays a clip from an entity. Use `bus` to route it (sfx | music | ui). 2D + 3D type: \"audioSource\" For dummies A speaker attached to an object. Fields Field Type Default clip asset(audio) — volume number 1 loop bool — autoplay bool — bus enum(sfx|music|ui) sfx Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('audioSource') . play(options) — restarts the clip, returns the audio handle stop()"},{"f":"component-audioListener.html","t":"audioListener","s":"Components","x":"Marker for the entity that hears the game. Currently a no-op placeholder (audio is not spatialised yet). 2D + 3D type: \"audioListener\" For dummies The ears of the player. Nothing to configure yet. Fields No fields. Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . In a scene"},{"f":"component-uiPanel.html","t":"uiPanel","s":"Components","x":"A background box in the DOM UI layer. UI type: \"uiPanel\" For dummies A rectangle on screen (HUD, menu background). Fields Field Type Default x number — y number — anchor enum(tl|t|tr|l|c|r|bl|b|br) tl w number — h number — screen string hud Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes All ui* components share positioning: anchor (tl,tr,bl,br; edge-anchored), x , y , w , h , and screen (a named layer you show/hide with game.ui.show(name) ). In a scene"},{"f":"component-uiLabel.html","t":"uiLabel","s":"Components","x":"Text in the DOM UI layer. UI type: \"uiLabel\" For dummies Words on top of the game (score, health). Fields Field Type Default text string — size int 16 color color — x number — y number — anchor enum(tl|t|tr|l|c|r|bl|b|br) tl w number — h number — screen string hud Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes Entity must be enabled to be visible. In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('uiLabel') . h.set(text) on `comp.h` Live-settable with entity.set('uiLabel', key, value) : (setter via handle) . Other keys are just stored."},{"f":"component-uiImage.html","t":"uiImage","s":"Components","x":"An image in the DOM UI layer. UI type: \"uiImage\" For dummies A picture on top of the game (icons, logos). Fields Field Type Default src asset(image) — x number — y number — anchor enum(tl|t|tr|l|c|r|bl|b|br) tl w number — h number — screen string hud Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . In a scene"},{"f":"component-uiBar.html","t":"uiBar","s":"Components","x":"A progress bar (0..1). UI type: \"uiBar\" For dummies A health bar or loading bar. Fields Field Type Default value number 1 color color — x number — y number — anchor enum(tl|t|tr|l|c|r|bl|b|br) tl w number — h number — screen string hud Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('uiBar') . h.set(0..1) on `comp.h`"},{"f":"component-uiButton.html","t":"uiButton","s":"Components","x":"A clickable button. Emits `click` on the entity, calls `onClick()` on its scripts and, if `event` is set, `game.events.emit(event)`. UI type: \"uiButton\" For dummies A button the player can press. Fields Field Type Default text string Button event string — x number — y number — anchor enum(tl|t|tr|l|c|r|bl|b|br) tl w number — h number — screen string hud Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . In a scene"},{"f":"component-path.html","t":"path","s":"Components","x":"A list of waypoints that can be sampled by distance — for patrols, moving platforms, camera rails. 2D + 3D type: \"path\" For dummies A dotted line on the map that characters can follow. Fields Field Type Default points array vec3 — loop bool — Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . Notes points is an array of [x,y,z]. loop closes the path. In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('path') . length — total length at(distance) → [x,y,z] (wraps if loop, clamps otherwise) points"},{"f":"component-spawner.html","t":"spawner","s":"Components","x":"Spawns a prefab every `interval` seconds at the entity (plus `offset` and random `jitter`), keeping at most `max` alive. 2D + 3D type: \"spawner\" For dummies A machine that keeps producing enemies or coins. Fields Field Type Default prefab asset(prefab) — interval number 1 max int — offset vec3 — jitter vec3 — autostart bool true Field list comes from the editor's inspector schema. Types: see attribute types . In a scene From a script Get the runtime component with this.entity.getComponent('spawner') . spawn() → Promise Entity on — boolean, set false to pause live — Set of alive spawned entities"},{"f":"project-format.html","t":"Project format","s":"Reference","x":"All files are UTF-8 JSON, pretty-printed (2 spaces), stable key order, trailing newline. Project folder # nord.project.json # Scene ( *.scene.json ) # Units: 2D = pixels, y down; 3D = metres, y up, left-handed (Babylon default). Rotation = degrees (Euler XYZ). Built-in components (v1) # sprite, animatedSprite, text, tilemap, particles, camera, light, mesh (primitive), model (glTF), material, rigidbody, collider, audioSource, audioListener, uiPanel/uiButton/uiLabel/uiImage/uiBar, trigger, path, spawner . Each component has a JSON schema { type, fields:[{name,type,default,min,max,enum,label}] } in runtime/core/components/*.schema.json ; the inspector is generated from schemas (same for script attributes ). Attribute types: number, int, bool, string, color, vec2, vec3, enum, entity, asset(image|audio|model|json|prefab), curve, array T . Prefab # Same as a scene with a single root entity: { format :1, root : {entity…} } . Asset metadata # Sidecar assets/foo.png.meta.json only when non-default: { type : spritesheet , frameWidth :32, frameHeight :32, anims :{ walk :{ frames :[0,3], fps :10, loop :true}}, filter : nearest , tags :[ character ] } . Library index ( library/INDEX.json ) # [{ id : controller.platformer2d , title : … , category : controllers , tags :[], tier :[ 2d ], file : … , description : … , attributes :[…], requires :[ physics.arcade ], demo : examples/… , source : original| upstream }] Attribute types # Used by script static attributes and component schemas (the inspector draws a widget for each). Type JSON value Options number float min , max , step , default , label int integer min , max bool true/false string text color #rrggbb (3–8 hex digits accepted) vec2 / vec3 [x, y] / [x, y, z] enum one of options options: [...] (script attributes) entity entity name or id asset project-relative path assetType : image audio model json prefab curve list of [t, value] points array list of values element type Invalid values fall back to the default with a console warning; numbers are clamped to min / max ; unknown attribute names warn."},{"f":"components.html","t":"Component index","s":"Reference","x":"Every built-in component, grouped by where it works. Click one for fields, notes and script access. 2D sprite A single image (or one frame of a spritesheet) drawn at the entity position. animatedSprite A spritesheet with named animations. Animations are declared in the texture's `.meta.json` (`anims`). text World-space text (Phaser Text). For HUD text prefer `uiLabel`. tilemap A grid of tiles from a tileset image. Loads Tiled JSON (`.tmj`/`.json`) or Nord simple maps (`layers[].data`). parallax A screen-filling background layer that scrolls slower than the camera for depth. 3D mesh A primitive 3D shape (box, sphere, cylinder, capsule, torus, plane, ground). model A glTF/GLB model instanced into the scene, with its animations. material PBR (or unlit) material applied to the meshes of this entity. skybox Cube-map sky. `path` is a folder containing px/nx/py/ny/pz/nz.png (e.g. `assets/skybox/day`). postProcess Full-screen effects on the active camera: bloom, FXAA/MSAA, vignette, tone mapping, grain, sharpen, chromatic aberration. 2D + 3D particles Particle emitter. 2D (Phaser emitter) and 3D (Babylon ParticleSystem) use the same component with tier-specific fields. camera 2D: configures the main camera (zoom, follow, bounds). 3D: creates a free (first-person) or orbit camera. light 2D: Phaser point light (turns on the light pipeline for all sprites). 3D: point / directional / spot / hemispheric light, optional shadows. rigidbody Makes the entity a physics body. Add a `collider` for its shape. collider Collision shape. Without an explicit `size` it is taken from the sprite/mesh bounds. trigger Shorthand for a collider with `trigger: true` — an invisible zone that fires `onTriggerEnter` / `onTriggerExit`. audioSource Plays a clip from an entity. Use `bus` to route it (sfx | music | ui). audioListener Marker for the entity that hears the game. Currently a no-op placeholder (audio is not spatialised yet). path A list of waypoints that can be sampled by distance — for patrols, moving platforms, camera rails. spawner Spawns a prefab every `interval` seconds at the entity (plus `offset` and random `jitter`), keeping at most `max` alive. UI uiPanel A background box in the DOM UI layer. uiLabel Text in the DOM UI layer. uiImage An image in the DOM UI layer. uiBar A progress bar (0..1). uiButton A clickable button. Emits `click` on the entity, calls `onClick()` on its scripts and, if `event` is set, `game.events.emit(event)`."},{"f":"cli.html","t":"Command line (nord)","s":"Reference","x":"The nord command-line tool ( crates/nord-cli ). Run nord --help or nord command --help for the same text. nord info Print engine info nord new Create a new project from a template Argument Type Default dir PathBuf — Directory to create (its name becomes the project name) -t, --template String html html | 2d | 3d | wasm-game | wasm-app, or a starter id from templates/ (e.g. platformer, fps-arena) nord doctor Check the toolchain and recommend a project tier nord thumbs Generate thumbnails for all project assets into .nord-cache/thumbs Argument Type Default project PathBuf . nord check Validate project + scenes (ids, missing files) Argument Type Default project PathBuf . nord serve Dev server with live reload and /api/* editor backend Argument Type Default project PathBuf . -p, --port u16 4173 nord build Build a static, offline web export (web-2d / web-3d / web-3d-pc / html / web-wasm-game / web-wasm-app) into a folder Argument Type Default project PathBuf . -t, --target String auto auto | html | web-2d | web-3d | web-3d-pc | web-wasm-game | web-wasm-app -o, --out Option PathBuf — Output folder (default: project /dist) --minify bool — Minify JS and JSON --single-file bool — One index.html with everything inlined (works from file://) --optimize bool — Lossless PNG recompression, WAV→MP3 when ffmpeg is installed --zip bool — Also write out .zip (for itch.io style uploads) nord build-rust Build a Rust (wasm-game / wasm-app) project for `nord serve`: cargo build → target/web/ Argument Type Default project PathBuf . --release bool — optimised build (slower, smaller) nord bundle-mac Wrap a web build (or a project, built first) into a macOS .app using the prebuilt nord-player shell Argument Type Default input PathBuf . A web build folder (nord build output) or a project folder --name Option String — -o, --out PathBuf . Output folder for the .app (default: current directory) --player Option PathBuf — nord-player binary or NordPlayer.app (default: auto-detect) --icon Option PathBuf — PNG for the app icon --dmg bool — Also create a .dmg (macOS only) nord bundle-portable Wrap a web build (or project) into a Windows / Linux desktop bundle (folder + zip / tar.gz) with the prebuilt nord-player Argument Type Default input PathBuf . --os String — windows | linux --name Option String — -o, --out PathBuf . --player Option PathBuf — nord-player binary for the target OS (default: auto-detect) --icon Option PathBuf — --no-archive bool — Skip the .zip / .tar.gz nord check-offline Fail if a directory (build output or project) references external http(s) hosts Argument Type Default dir PathBuf . --allow Vec String — Extra allowed host (repeatable); `.offline-allow` in the dir is also read nord vendor Vendored library tools Argument Type Default --verify bool — Verify sha256 of vendored files against vendor/VENDOR.json"},{"f":"library-catalog.html","t":"Library catalog","s":"Reference","x":"132 ready-made scripts, prefabs, shaders and app kits in library/ . Insert one from the editor's Library tab, or copy the file into your project. ai (9) Item Kind Tier What it does A* pathfinding (grid) ai.astar script 2d A* on a boolean grid. Import { findPath } from './ai-astar.js': findPath(blocked(x,y)→bool, [sx,sy], [gx,gy], {w,h,diagonal}) → [[x,y]…] or null. Default script walks its entity along a path to the clicked tile on a grid with solid-tagged obstacles. attributes: tile grid speed obstacleTag diagonal library/scripts/ai-astar.js Behaviour tree (lite) ai.behaviour-tree script 2d, 3d Minimal behaviour tree: sequence/selector/inverter/action/condition nodes returning 'success'|'failure'|'running'. Import { sequence, selector, action, cond, run } from './ai-behaviour-tree.js'. The default script runs a sample tree: flee when the player is near, else wander. attributes: speed fearRadius target library/scripts/ai-behaviour-tree.js Boss with phases ai.boss-phases script 2d Boss pattern controller: phases switch at health thresholds (phases: [{at:0.66, move:'strafe', fire:{rate,burst,spread}}]). Reads the sibling Health script, strafes/charges and fires bullet prefabs. Emits 'boss:phase' and 'boss:dead'. attributes: bullet phases speed bulletSpeed target library/scripts/ai-boss.js Enemy AI: chase ai.chase script 2d, 3d Chases the nearest tagged target when it is within sight range, gives up beyond lose range, optionally keeps distance (kiting). Contact damage via the target's 'damage' event. attributes: target speed sight lose keepDistance contactDamage contactCooldown library/scripts/ai-chase.js Flocking (boids) ai.flock script 2d Boids steering: separation, alignment, cohesion with all entities that share a tag, optional attraction to a target. Add to each member; all members use the shared tag. attributes: tag speed radius separation alignment cohesion attract turn library/scripts/ai-flock.js State machine (FSM) ai.fsm script 2d, 3d Tiny finite-state-machine helper: new FSM({idle:{enter,update,exit,next}}, 'idle'). Import it from this file in your own scripts: `import { FSM } from './ai-fsm.js'`. The default-exported script demonstrates it by cycling idle→walk with a timer. attributes: idleTime walkTime speed library/scripts/ai-fsm.js Enemy AI: patrol ai.patrol script 2d Walks back and forth over a distance, turning at walls/ledge ends (checks ground ahead with overlapBox) and pauses at the ends. attributes: speed distance pause ledgeCheck library/scripts/ai-patrol.js Enemy AI: shooter / turret ai.shooter script 2d Aims at the nearest tagged target in range and fires a bullet prefab at a fixed rate with optional spread and burst; leads moving targets if 'lead' is on. attributes: target bullet range rate speed burst spread lead rotate library/scripts/ai-shooter.js Follow waypoint path ai.waypoints script 2d, 3d Moves along a 'path' component on this or another entity (by name) at a speed; loops or ping-pongs. Great for tower-defense creeps and patrols. attributes: pathEntity speed mode faceDirection library/scripts/ai-waypoints.js apps (15) Item Kind Tier What it does Audio player + visualiser app.audio-player app-kit html Playlist player with play/pause, seek, volume and a WebAudio frequency-bar visualiser. mount(root,{tracks:[{title,src}]}). library/apps/audio-player.js Calculator app.calculator app-kit html Four-function calculator with percent, sign and keyboard input. Safe evaluation (no eval). library/apps/calculator.js Clipboard manager (desktop) app.clipboard-manager app-kit html Keeps a searchable history of copied text (pin, dedupe, clear), puts any entry back on the clipboard with one click, adds a tray icon + menu, and a global shortcut (Ctrl+Alt+V) that cycles through the history. Needs permissions clipboard:'read', tray, globalShortcuts, notifications. library/apps/clipboardmanager.js Code editor (desktop) app.code-editor app-kit html A real code editor for Nord desktop apps: file tree, tabs, syntax highlighting (JS/TS/HTML/CSS/JSON/Markdown/Rust/GLSL/TOML/PHP/Python/Go/C/C++/C#/Java/Kotlin/Swift/Ruby/Lua/Shell/SQL/YAML/XML), find replace, multi-cursor, folding, autocomplete, go to line, quick open, themes, autosave, external-change detection, session restore. Uses window.nord.desktop (files, dialogs, menu) and the vendored CodeMirror 6 bundle; in a browser it runs on the virtual app-data files. library/apps/codeeditor.js Dashboard + charts app.dashboard app-kit html KPI tiles plus tiny dependency-free canvas charts (line, bar, donut). Export lineChart/barChart/donutChart for reuse. mount(root, {data}) with {kpis:[{label,value,delta}], series:{labels,values}}. library/apps/dashboard.js Notes (desktop) app.desktop-notes app-kit html Markdown notes as real files in the app data folder: search, drag-to-reorder pinned order, autosave, import/export through native dialogs, reminders via system notifications, dark-mode follow and en/de UI. Uses window.nord.desktop (fs, store, dialog, notify) and the _ux helpers. library/apps/desktopnotes.js File organiser (desktop) app.file-organizer app-kit html Pick a folder, see its files grouped by type in a virtualised table (handles 10k+ files), preview and apply 'sort into folders' by type or by month with full undo, and find duplicate files by size + SHA-256. Uses window.nord.desktop fs + dialogs; every change is previewed first. library/apps/fileorganizer.js JSON / CSV viewer app.json-viewer app-kit html Paste or open a JSON or CSV file: JSON renders as a collapsible tree, CSV as a sortable table. mount(root, {text}). library/apps/json-viewer.js Kanban board app.kanban app-kit html Three-column board (To do / Doing / Done) with pointer-based drag drop (works in webviews), add/delete cards, persisted in localStorage. library/apps/kanban.js Markdown editor (desktop) app.markdown-editor app-kit html Split-view Markdown editor: CodeMirror 6 on the left, live sanitised preview (vendored marked) on the right, open/save through native d"},{"f":"templates-examples.html","t":"Templates & examples","s":"Reference","x":"Start a project from a template with nord new my-game -t id (or the project hub). Examples are complete games in examples/ — open them in the editor to see how they are built. Starter templates Desktop app: clipboard manager desktop-clipboard-manager · html — Clipboard history with search/pin, tray icon + menu, global shortcut Ctrl+Alt+V, badge count. Desktop app: code editor desktop-code-editor · html — Tabs, file tree, syntax highlighting (JS/TS/HTML/CSS/JSON/MD/Rust/GLSL/TOML), find replace, multi-cursor, quick open — CodeMirror 6 + window.nord.desktop. Desktop app: notes desktop-notes · html — Notes as files in the app data folder: search, reorder, autosave, import/export dialogs, reminders (notifications), dark-mode follow, en/de. Desktop app: file organiser desktop-file-organizer · html — Pick a folder, sort files into folders by type/month with preview + undo, find duplicates (SHA-256), virtual table for 10k+ files. Desktop app: markdown editor desktop-markdown-editor · html — Split view CodeMirror + live sanitised preview (marked), open/save/export HTML, drag-drop, file association for .md. UI app (Todo) ui-app · html — Plain HTML/JS app with a tiny DOM kit — todo list with persistence. Swap in any library app kit. Desktop app: local web server desktop-web-server · html — Serve a folder over HTTP: live reload, SPA fallback, CORS, directory listing, request log + graph — window.nord.desktop.server. Endless one-button (Flappy) endless-runner · 2d — Scrolling obstacles, one-button control, score and restart. FPS arena fps-arena · 3d — First-person controller (mouse look, sprint, crouch, jump), hit-scan weapon, destructible targets. Idle / clicker idle-clicker · 2d — Currency, upgrades with cost scaling, per-second income, offline progress, autosave. Puzzle (Match-3) puzzle-match3 · 2d — Swap-and-match core: board, cascades, refill, score and move limit. Card game (Memory) card-game · 2d — Card grid with flip animation, matching logic, move counter and win state — DOM UI based. Platformer platformer · 2d — Tilemap level, hero with coyote time/variable jump/dash, enemies you can stomp, coins, checkpoints, boss, HUD. 3D platformer platformer-3d · 3d — Third-person-ish 3D platformer: coyote jump, double jump, moving platform, gems, respawn. Racing (time-trial) racing · 3d — Arcade car, checkpoints, lap timer, best lap saved, chase camera. Endless runner runner · 2d — Auto-running character with jump/duck, obstacles, speed ramp and distance score. Grid game (Snake) puzzle-grid · 2d — Tile-based game loop: timed steps, grid entities, food, game over. Shooter (Space) shooter · 2d — Side-scrolling shooter: player ship, bullets, waves of enemies, score and game over. Top-down adventure topdown-adventure · 2d — Rooms, NPC dialogue, inventory, key + door, pickups, chasing enemies, HUD. Tower defense tower-defense · 2d — Path, waves of creeps, click-to-build towers with range and fire rate, gold and lives. Visual novel visual-novel · 2d — Branching dialogue from a JSON file (library dialogue script): speakers, typewriter text, choices. Rust 2D game rust-2d-game · wasm-game — macroquad + rapier2d runtime, scenes in JSON, scripts in Rust (builds to WebAssembly). Rust egui app rust-egui-app · wasm-app — eframe/egui app that runs natively and in the browser (wasm-bindgen, no trunk). Rust Bevy game (optional) rust-bevy-game · wasm-app — Slim Bevy 0.16 app (WebGL2): spinning cube starter. Runs natively and in the browser via the wasm-app export path; own ECS, no Nord scenes. Examples 2048 Arrow keys / WASD slide the tiles. Built with the DOM UI layer only (this.ui), so no sprites needed. Best score is saved. altimu A one-area proof of concept of an Ultima V game set in Ultima VII's Trinsic: modern hand-pixelled sprites, Ultima V rules. app-audio Plain HTML + ES modules, no build step and no engine runtime. The app is library/apps/audio-player.js (copied to app.js): mount(rootElement). app-calculator Plain HTML + ES modules, no build step and no engine runtime. The app is library/apps/calculator.js (copied to app.js): mount(rootElement). app-clipboard-manager A Nord desktop app: plain HTML + ES modules using window.nord.desktop (files, dialogs, menus, ...). Permissions are declared in nord.project.json → desktop.permissions (least privilege; see docs/DESKTOP_APPS.md). app-code-editor A Nord desktop app: plain HTML + ES modules using window.nord.desktop (files, dialogs, menus, ...). Permissions are declared in nord.project.json → desktop.permissions (least privilege; see docs/DESKTOP_APPS.md). app-dashboard Plain HTML + ES modules, no build step and no engine runtime. The app is library/apps/dashboard.js (copied to app.js): mount(rootElement). app-desktop-notes A Nord desktop app: plain HTML + ES modules using window.nord.desktop (files, dialogs, menus, ...). Permissions are declared in nord.project.json → desktop.permissions (least privilege; see docs/DESKTOP_APPS.md). app-file-organizer A Nord desktop app: plain HTML + ES modules using window.nord.desktop (files, dialogs, menus, ...). Permissions are declared in nord.project.json → desktop.permissions (least privilege; see docs/DESKTOP_APPS.md). app-json-viewer Plain HTML + ES modules, no build step and no engine runtime. The app is library/apps/json-viewer.js (copied to app.js): mount(rootElement). app-kanban Plain HTML + ES modules, no build step and no engine runtime. The app is library/apps/kanban.js (copied to app.js): mount(rootElement). app-markdown-editor A Nord desktop app: plain HTML + ES modules using window.nord.desktop (files, dialogs, menus, ...). Permissions are declared in nord.project.json → desktop.permissions (least privilege; see docs/DESKTOP_APPS.md). app-notes Plain HTML + ES modules, no build step and no engine runtime. The app is library/apps/notes.js (copied to app.js): mount(rootElement). app-paint Plain HTML + ES modules, no build step and no engine runtime. The app is library/apps/paint.js (copied to app.js): mount(rootEle"},{"f":"rust-tier-spec.html","t":"Rust tier spec","s":"Reference","x":"Two project types besides JS: wasm-game (macroquad + rapier2d, same scene JSON as the JS tier) and wasm-app (eframe/egui). Both run natively ( cargo run ) and in the browser. Decision record: ADR-019. Project layout # The path dependency ( ../../crates/nord-runtime-wasm ) is relative to the engine checkout; New Project / hub example copies rewrite it to the absolute engine path. Building # What Command dev build (writes target/web/game.wasm or app.js + app_bg.wasm ) nord build-rust [dir] [--release] / editor Build Rust web export (static folder + optional zip) nord export dir --target web-wasm-game | web-wasm-app [--optimize] [--zip] native run cargo run in the project folder Needs rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown . wasm-app also needs the wasm-bindgen CLI **with the same version as the wasm-bindgen crate in your Cargo.lock** ( cargo install wasm-bindgen-cli --version X ). --optimize uses wasm-opt (binaryen) when installed. Script API ( nord_runtime_wasm ) # Hooks: start, update, fixed_update(60 Hz), on_destroy, on_collision_enter/exit, on_trigger_enter/exit, on_event(name, value), ui(egui) . Ctx : pos/set_pos, velocity/set_velocity(x?,y?), impulse, grounded, find/find_tag, has_tag, destroy, spawn_prefab(path,pos), emit(name,value), play(sound,vol), set_text, set_tint, play_anim, flip_x, overlap_box, camera_shake/follow/zoom, input() (axis/vec/down/pressed/released/pointer), rand(), restart() . Everything the JSON mentions (textures, sounds, prefabs, tilemaps) is preloaded; code-only assets are listed with Registry::prefab/sound/texture . Supported components (2D subset) # sprite, animatedSprite (sheet meta + anims), text , tilemap (+ static collider runs for collides ), parallax , particles (CPU emitter), camera (follow/lerp/bounds/zoom), rigidbody (static/dynamic/kinematic), collider (box/circle/capsule, trigger), trigger , uiLabel/uiBar/uiButton/uiPanel (egui), audioSource (autoplay). Not yet: lights, 3D, spawner/path components, joints, animation events. Units: pixels, y down, degrees; physics runs in metres at 32 px/m. Collider offsets are relative to the entity centre (sprites are centre-origin). Limits / gotchas # Audio is .wav/.ogg only (macroquad). Export does not convert mp3. macroquad&#39;s JS bundle logs a harmless register_plugin is not defined error (quad_net section). The runtime needs the page to serve nord.project.json , scenes and assets next to index.html (the export does). Bevy template (optional, M8-06) # rust-bevy-game (hub / nord new -t rust-bevy-game ) is a wasm-app project whose src/main.rs is a slim Bevy app (WebGL2, canvas #the_canvas_id ). It uses the same nord build-rust / nord export --target web-wasm-app path as the egui template. Bevy has its own ECS and does not read Nord scenes; prefer rust-2d-game for small games."},{"f":"export-targets-spec.html","t":"Export targets spec","s":"Reference","x":"nord export project --target t [--out dist/] [--minify] [--single-file] Target Output Runtime shipped Notes html folder none (your own HTML/JS) simple games/apps; optional nord-lite.js helpers web-2d folder nord.js + Phaser (+ chosen plugins) lib/phaser.min.js , assets copied/atlased web-3d folder nord.js + Babylon packages + Havok wasm only packages used by scene are copied web-3d-pc folder nord.js + PlayCanvas engine (optional 4th tier, ADR-021) vendor/playcanvas/playcanvas.min.js (~2.5 MB); no physics , no particles/postProcess/skybox yet web-wasm-game folder nord_game.wasm + mq_js_bundle.js + index.html built with cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown + wasm-opt -Oz if present web-wasm-app folder eframe wasm (+ JS glue via wasm-bindgen ) mac-app Name.app (+ optional .dmg ) NordPlayer + any of the above uses prebuilt player; ad-hoc signed zip name.zip any web target zipped for itch.io-style upload Implemented (M4) # nord build project [-t auto|html|web-2d|web-3d|web-3d-pc] [-o dir] [--minify] [--single-file] [--optimize] [--zip] (alias nord export ), crate nord-export ; editor: Project → Export… . web-wasm-game (M5-05, verified) builds the Rust project with cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown (flags: --allow-undefined via --config ), copies game.wasm , the vendored mq_js_bundle.js and project data/assets, writes a relative index.html ; --optimize runs wasm-opt -Oz if installed. web-wasm-app (M5-06) uses wasm-bindgen --target web . Rust-tier scripts are registry names (no file check in validation); audio must be .wav/.ogg. --minify / --single-file run tools/export-bundle.mjs (Node + vendored esbuild-wasm ). nord bundle-mac project|dist [--player nord-player|NordPlayer.app] [--name] [-o dir] [--icon png] [--dmg] assembles Name .app ( Contents/Resources/game/ + Info.plist + generated AppIcon.icns, ad-hoc codesign on macOS); the player is crates/nord-player (wry+tao, --features shell , macOS-only to build). Hosting: HOSTING.md. Not done: texture-atlas packing and lossy PNG quantisation (M4-11). Output layout (web) # Must work from: any static server, python3 -m http.server , Netlify/itch/GitHub Pages, and file:// for the --single-file or file-safe mode (inline JSON + scripts, no fetch of local files). Build steps (web-2d/3d) # Load project, validate (nord-core). 2. Resolve used components → libs. 3. Optimise assets (optional: png quantise, audio→ogg/mp3). Emit nord.json + copy files. 5. Generate index.html from runtime/templates/*.html . 6. Minify JS (optional, vendored esbuild-wasm or none). check-offline . 8. Report sizes. Tier auto-recommendation ( nord doctor ) # Only DOM/canvas, 3 scenes, no physics → html 2D with sprites/physics/tilemaps → web-2d Any 3D → web-3d User picked Rust, or needs ~5k active physics bodies/CPU heavy sims → web-wasm-game Tool/editor/dashboard in Rust → web-wasm-app Mac wrapper # See ARCHITECTURE §7. NordPlayer = wry+tao shell, no devtools in release, window config from nord.json.display , fullscreen toggle (Cmd+Ctrl+F), persistent localStorage in app data dir."},{"f":"hosting-spec.html","t":"Hosting notes","s":"Reference","x":"nord build project writes a static folder (default project /dist ): index.html , nord.json , scenes/ scripts/ assets/ , runtime/ , vendor/ . Nothing is fetched from the internet — upload the folder anywhere that serves static files. Where How Tested Local, quick cd dist python3 -m http.server 8000 → http://localhost:8000 yes ( node tools/e2e.mjs export-python-http : 3D build incl. Havok .wasm ) Double-click / file:// build with --single-file ; index.html works from the file system (ES modules and fetch do not work from file:// , so folder builds need a server) yes ( export-single-2d/3d , loaded from file:// ) itch.io --zip , upload the zip as HTML , tick This file will be played in the browser , set the viewport to the game&#39;s display size. Use the folder build (not single-file) zip layout tested, upload is manual GitHub Pages commit dist/ to a gh-pages branch (or /docs ) and enable Pages. Paths are relative, so project sub-paths ( user.github.io/repo/ ) work relative paths verified; Pages itself untested nginx root /srv/game; — make sure .wasm is served as application/wasm (default mime.types has it) and .js as text/javascript mime requirements covered by the python test Netlify / Cloudflare Pages / S3 upload the folder; no server config needed untested Notes # Every output is checked for external http(s):// references (the offline guarantee ); the build fails if your own files contain one. Allow a host on purpose with a .offline-allow file in the project. Audio needs a user gesture in browsers; the runtime unlocks it on the first click/key. nord.json lists every file with size + sha256 (cache busting / integrity checks). --single-file inlines scripts, scenes and assets as data: URIs: great for small games, big for large asset sets. UI fonts stay as separate files in vendor/fonts/ . macOS app: nord bundle-mac my-game wraps the build with the prebuilt nord-player (see EXPORT_TARGETS.md)."},{"f":"desktop-apps-spec.html","t":"Desktop apps spec","s":"Reference","x":"Status: implemented (M9) with the defaults chosen below (owner can still veto: plain DOM UI, JSON store + optional SQLite, portable zip/tar.gz first). See ADR-020 in DECISIONS.md . What exists now # Crate crates/nord-desktop : caps (manifest, audit, grants), paths , fs (scoped, atomic, polling watcher), store (+ sqlite feature, migrations), services (dialogs/notifications/clipboard/shell/secrets traits; native = rfd/notify-rust/arboard/opener, keychain = keyring, Headless for tests), process (+ http ), server (static HTTP server), lifecycle (single instance, argv/deep links, autostart), window (menu/tray/window specs → HostCommand s), bridge (JSON dispatcher = the whole JS API). JS client runtime/desktop/nord-desktop.js (+ .d.ts ): ipc / http / web transports. nord serve mounts /__desktop/call (needs the x-nord header; same permissions). Player shell crates/nord-player ( --features shell ): IPC, multi-window, native menu bar, tray, global shortcuts, drag drop, single instance, window-state persistence, quit guard. Rust: nord_app_wasm::desktop (+ ux ). Packaging: nord bundle-mac (+ file types/URL schemes), nord bundle-portable --os windows|linux , docs/PACKAGING.md . Editor: Project ▸ Desktop app permissions… ; nord-core validates the manifest. Examples/templates: code editor, web server, notes, file organiser, clipboard manager, markdown editor (JS) and Rust notes. Verified on Linux (webkit2gtk under Xvfb, headless Chromium); macOS/Windows paths compile-checked or CI-only — see KNOWN_ISSUES. Goal # Build real desktop apps (notes, file organiser, clipboard manager, markdown editor…) from a Nord project, in either tier: JS/web tier — HTML/JS app (Phaser UI or plain DOM) running in a native window, calling window.nord.desktop.* . Rust tier — nord-app-wasm (eframe/egui) app calling a Rust Desktop API. Same project, same export pipeline; the app runs in the browser too with graceful fallbacks. Decision: shell = reuse nord-player (wry+tao); Rust apps run native egui — both on one backend crate # Option Verdict A. Extend nord-player (wry webview + tao window) with an IPC bridge Chosen for JS apps. Already built, already bundles .app ; WebView2 / WKWebView / WebKitGTK per OS. Adds IPC + a few tao features (menus, tray, multi-window) B. Rust apps as native eframe (not wasm in a webview) Chosen for Rust apps. nord-app-wasm::launch already runs native; add the Desktop services to Ctx . No webview, no IPC C. Tauri Rejected (ADR-013: we own the shell; Tauri adds a framework and a JS API we don&#39;t control) D. Everything in the webview, Rust app as wasm inside it Rejected: slower, needs IPC for every call, loses native text input/IME So: one Rust crate nord-desktop holds all services (fs, dialogs, notifications, clipboard, store, paths, shell-open, windows/tray/menu helpers where toolkit-independent). The JS bridge (in nord-player ) and the Rust API (in nord-app-wasm ) are two thin front-ends over it. No logic is duplicated; permission checks live in the crate, so both tiers are equally sandboxed. Bridge protocol (JS) # Page → host: window.ipc.postMessage(JSON {id, method: fs.read , params}) ; host → page: evaluate_script( __nord_reply(id, ok, payload) ) . Promise-based wrapper in runtime/desktop/nord-desktop.js (injected via with_initialization_script ; same file used in the editor&#39;s Play, where calls go through nord-serve HTTP instead — ADR-014 style — with the same capability checks, so dev ≈ exported). Events host → page (file watch, menu click, tray click, deep link, drop): nord.desktop.on( menu , fn) . Only pages loaded from nord://game/ may call the bridge (origin check); remote URLs never get it. Binary data is base64 (small) or via nord:// blob URLs for large reads (decide in M9-10). Capability model (least privilege) # nord.project.json : Default = nothing except appData storage and dialogs. Everything else must be declared; the export bakes the manifest into nord.json ; the host never widens it at runtime. fs roots are symbolic: appData (per-app dir), documents , downloads , temp , picked (paths the user chose through an Open/Save dialog this run or persisted as bookmarks). No arbitrary absolute paths from the app. Path rules (implemented once in nord-desktop::fs ): canonicalise → must be inside a granted root → reject .. , symlink escapes, Windows device names/UNC/ADS; tests for each (M9-16). Sensitive grants ( process , net , globalShortcuts , clipboard: read ) ask on first use (native confirm dialog), remembered per app; audit log in appData ( audit.log ); editor shows/reviews them (M9-12). macOS: App Sandbox is optional; when signed with sandbox entitlement, picked uses security-scoped bookmarks (M9-03). Unsandboxed ad-hoc builds (default) rely on our own caps only — our caps are not an OS sandbox ; documented honestly (the app&#39;s own code is trusted, the page content is the thing constrained). What real apps need — checklist (→ task) # Need Task Tier support Read/write/list/watch files, user-picked folders M9-03 JS, Rust Open/save/folder/message dialogs, recent files M9-04 JS, Rust Notifications, dock/taskbar badge + progress M9-05 JS, Rust Settings, key-value + structured store, secrets in keychain M9-06 JS, Rust Clipboard, drag drop, file associations, deep links, single instance M9-07 JS, Rust Multi-window, menu bar, context menu, tray, global shortcuts, window state M9-08 JS (tao); Rust (eframe viewports + tray-icon / muda ) Timers/threads, HTTP, process spawn allow-list, quit confirm, autostart M9-09 JS, Rust Typed API ( d.ts ), docs, same in dev export M9-10/11 — Permission manifest, prompts, editor UI M9-12 — Packaging mac/win/linux, icons, versions, optional updater M9-13 — IME, hi-DPI, dark-mode follow, keyboard nav, i18n, undo helpers M9-14 — Templates/examples M9-15 — Tests + CI matrix M9-16 — Crate/library picks (all permissive, pinned in M9-02) # rfd (dialogs), notify-rust or platform calls (notifications), arboard (clipboard), directories (paths), notify (fs watc"},{"f":"packaging.html","t":"Packaging","s":"Reference","x":"Nord does resource swap , not per-app compilation: a prebuilt nord-player (wry + tao shell, plus the nord-desktop backend) is copied and your exported web build is placed next to it as game/ . Build the player once per OS: cargo build --release -p nord-player --features shell (macOS: WKWebView; Windows: WebView2 — preinstalled on Win 10/11; Linux: libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev libgtk-3-dev libayatana-appindicator3-dev libxdo-dev ). Target Command Output macOS nord bundle-mac project [--dmg] [--icon icon.png] Name .app (+ .dmg ), ad-hoc signed on a Mac Windows nord bundle-portable project --os windows --player nord-player.exe Name / Name .exe + game/ + icon.ico + register.reg , Name -windows.zip Linux nord bundle-portable project --os linux --player nord-player name / name + game/ + name .desktop + name .png , name -linux.tar.gz Web nord export project static folder / zip nord bundle-portable can run on any OS (it only copies files); the player binary must be built for the target OS (CI does this, see .github/workflows/ci.yml ). Engine itself: ./build.sh → dist/Nord Engine.app , dist/windows/ , dist/linux/ . Manifest fields that affect packaging ( nord.project.json → desktop ) # macOS → CFBundleURLTypes / CFBundleDocumentTypes in Info.plist (double-click / open -a / nordnotes://… reach the app as the open event + app.launch() ). Linux → MimeType= + x-scheme-handler/… in the .desktop file; install it to ~/.local/share/applications/ and run update-desktop-database . Windows → register.reg (HKCU). Edit the C:\\Path\\To\\… install path, double-click to register file types and URL schemes. An installer should do this itself. Version shown in the bundle comes from nord.json.version (falls back to the engine version). Icon: --icon , else icon.png in the project, else the Nord logo. macOS: signing, notarisation, dmg # nord bundle-mac / build.sh sign ad-hoc ( codesign -s - ): fine on your own Mac; a downloaded copy needs right-click → Open. To distribute: Join the Apple Developer Program; create a Developer ID Application certificate (Xcode → Settings → Accounts → Manage Certificates). Sign with the hardened runtime (needed for notarisation; the player needs no special entitlements — it uses WKWebView and no JIT of its own): codesign --force --deep --options runtime --timestamp --sign Developer ID Application: NAME (TEAMID) App.app Zip and notarise: ditto -c -k --keepParent App.app App.zip , then xcrun notarytool submit App.zip --apple-id you@x.com --team-id TEAMID --password app-specific-password --wait (store credentials once with xcrun notarytool store-credentials ). Staple: xcrun stapler staple App.app ; check: spctl -a -vvv -t install App.app . dmg: hdiutil create -volname App -srcfolder App.app -ov -format UDZO App.dmg , then sign + notarise + staple the dmg too ( codesign --sign Developer ID… App.dmg ; notarytool submit App.dmg … ; stapler staple App.dmg ). Apps that read user folders (Documents, Downloads…) trigger the OS permission prompts automatically; add NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription -style keys only if you extend Info.plist yourself. Sandboxing ( com.apple.security.app-sandbox ) is optional and not enabled: our permission manifest is an application-level guard, not an OS sandbox (ADR-020). Not verifiable on Linux; steps follow Apple&#39;s documented flow — report problems. Windows # Portable zip works as is (WebView2 runtime ships with Windows 10/11; for older systems bundle Microsoft&#39;s evergreen bootstrapper). SmartScreen warns for unsigned executables. Sign with signtool sign /fd SHA256 /tr http://timestamp.digicert.com /td SHA256 /a App.exe (a code-signing certificate is needed). Installer (optional, later): Inno Setup / WiX wrapping the folder; write the register.reg entries from there; autostart is done by the app via app.setAutostart() (HKCU\\…\\Run). Linux # tar.gz runs anywhere with webkit2gtk-4.1 + gtk3 installed. .desktop + icon → ~/.local/share/applications , ~/.local/share/icons . AppImage (optional): put the folder in an AppDir ( AppRun → the exe), add the .desktop and icon, run appimagetool ; .deb : dpkg-deb --build of /opt/ name + /usr/share/applications/ name .desktop (depends: libwebkit2gtk-4.1-0, libgtk-3-0, libayatana-appindicator3-1 ). Wayland: global shortcuts and tray icons depend on the compositor; X11/XWayland always works. Auto-update (strategy only, opt-in) # Not built in. Recommended: the app checks a JSON file with desktop.http.fetch (declare the host in permissions.net ), downloads the new bundle with the same API into appData , verifies a SHA-256 from the signed manifest, then asks the user ( dialog.confirm ) and reveals/launches it ( shell.reveal ). Never self-modify a signed bundle in place."},{"f":"appendix.html","t":"Appendix","s":"Appendix","x":"Vendored versions # Phaser 4.2.1 · Babylon.js 9.30.0 (+loaders, gui, materials, post-process, procedural textures, serializers) · Havok 1.3.14 · PlayCanvas 2.23.2 (optional) · CodeMirror 6 · marked 18.1.0 · Lucide icons · macroquad 0.4.16 · fonts (Inter, Plus Jakarta Sans, IBM Plex, JetBrains Mono, Fira Code). The authoritative list with sha256 hashes is vendor/VENDOR.json ; verify with nord vendor --verify . Licences # Nord Engine is MIT. Third-party code and assets (Phaser MIT, Babylon Apache-2.0, Havok wrapper MIT, Lucide ISC, Inter/JetBrains Mono OFL-1.1, Kenney pack CC0) are listed in THIRD_PARTY.md . Placeholder art and audio are generated by this repo (MIT). Credit line: Crowelian 2026 + Sonnet . Troubleshooting # Symptom Fix Blank page after export Folder builds must be served over http. Use python3 -m http.server , or build with --single-file . 3D physics missing on a host Serve .wasm as application/wasm . No sound Browsers require a click/key first; sounds are queued until then. Script change not applied Imports of a script are not hot-reloaded — reload the game. nord check-offline fails A file mentions an http(s):// host. Remove it, or add the host to .offline-allow . Rust build fails to link rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown ; for apps install wasm-bindgen-cli at the lock-file version. Editor webview blank (macOS) Use Open in browser in the Game tab and report it — the embedded webview is the least-tested part ( docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md ). FAQ # Do I have to learn Phaser or Babylon? No. Nord&#39;s API covers them; entity.native is only an escape hatch. Can I use TypeScript? Not yet (planned via esbuild-wasm). Can I make a Windows/Linux app? The web export runs anywhere; nord bundle-mac (macOS) and nord bundle-portable --os windows|linux wrap it with the native player; real desktop apps (files, dialogs, tray…) are covered in Desktop apps . Windows/Linux builds are CI-built; owner verification pending. Where do I report bugs / what is known broken? Known issues . Can I use my own assets? Yes — just keep them under assets/ and make sure you may redistribute them. Where the source of truth lives # Task list: PRD.json / task.md . Decisions: Design decisions . Behaviour specs: docs/*.md in the repository."},{"f":"decisions.html","t":"Design decisions","s":"Appendix","x":"Append new decisions at the bottom. Format: context → decision → consequences. Status: accepted | superseded. ADR-001 Editor = Tauri v2 + web UI (PCUI), not egui — SUPERSEDED by ADR-013 # Context. Owner asked egui as engine or copy from examples, if better and use some better UI , plus a full code editor with CodeMirror, vendored , thumbnails, docs with live code. Decision. Editor UI is HTML/JS (PCUI from PlayCanvas editor, CodeMirror 6, Lucide icons) inside a Tauri v2 shell with Rust backend. The viewport reuses Babylon/Phaser = true WYSIWYG. Consequences. + CodeMirror native, + docs/tutorials in the same stack, + testable in Chromium, Win/Linux bundles come free. − Not a pure Rust UI. egui is still used for the Rust app runtime (ADR-010). Veto path: swap shell to eframe+wry; backend contract (ARCHITECTURE §4) stays. ADR-002 Three runtime tiers sharing one scene JSON — accepted # Phaser (2D JS), Babylon (3D JS), Rust/WASM (macroquad games, egui apps), plus a plain-HTML tier. Owner asked for Phaser+Babylon vendored and for wasm+webgl. Conformance examples must run on every tier that supports them. ADR-003 Scripting = JavaScript first — accepted # JS scripts hot-reload instantly and are what the huge snippet library is written in. Rust scripts only for wasm tiers. TypeScript optional later via vendored typescript /esbuild-wasm (not now). ADR-004 Everything vendored, no CDN — accepted # vendor/ committed; offline scanner enforces. Fonts too (bundle Inter + JetBrains Mono, OFL). ADR-005 Upstream repos are harvested, not forked — accepted # tools/fetch-upstreams.sh makes shallow/sparse clones into gitignored third_party/ ; useful files are copied into library/ , examples/ , docs/ , editor/ with attribution in THIRD_PARTY.md ; UPSTREAM.lock.json records SHAs. Do not vendor whole Godot (≈1 GB+). Godot/Defold UIs are not reused . ADR-006 PRD.json is the source of truth for tasks — accepted # task.md is generated by node tools/tasks.mjs render . Mark work with node tools/tasks.mjs done ID . CI/AI can run node tools/tasks.mjs check to detect drift. ADR-007 No JS build step for editor/runtime — accepted # ES modules + import maps. Only exception: one-off bundles (CodeMirror) produced by tools/vendor.mjs with esbuild and committed. ADR-008 Mac wrapper via prebuilt player shell — accepted # Prebuilt NordPlayer.app (wry+tao) + resource swap (ARCHITECTURE §7). Avoids needing Xcode/Rust on the user&#39;s machine per game. ADR-009 Physics choices — accepted # 2D: Phaser Arcade (fast, simple) + Matter (rich). 3D: Havok ( @babylonjs/havok , wasm). Rust: rapier2d (rapier3d later). ADR-010 Rust/WASM: macroquad for games, eframe/egui for apps — accepted # macroquad: tiny wasm, WebGL1/2, audio, no wasm-bindgen needed. Bevy is reference/examples source only (large wasm; maybe an M8 template). raylib: examples used as porting source for macroquad samples (raylib itself is C). ADR-011 Licences — accepted # Own code MIT. Vendored libs keep licences (Phaser MIT, Babylon Apache-2, CodeMirror MIT, PCUI MIT, Lucide ISC, rapier Apache-2, macroquad MIT/Apache, egui MIT/Apache). Godot icons CC-BY-4.0 need attribution if used. Defold ships a custom licence (Apache-based with extra terms) → inspiration only until reviewed. Re-verify each LICENSE at fetch time ( fetch-upstreams.sh prints them). ADR-012 Platform strategy — accepted # Develop for aarch64-apple-darwin; use camino / dirs / std::path correctly, no hard-coded / . CI: macos-14 (Apple Silicon) first; Windows/Linux jobs added in M8 (allowed to fail until then). ADR-013 Editor = native Rust egui (eframe + egui_dock) + embedded wry viewport; no Tauri — accepted (owner decision) # Context. Owner prefers a native, lightweight Rust editor over a webview app, and asked for a full code editor. Games are JS (Phaser/Babylon) so a webview is needed for viewport/preview regardless; Tauri only added a web UI shell. Decision. nord-editor = eframe (wgpu/Metal) + egui_dock + egui_extras (images, SVG icons, syntect). The central viewport/Play/Docs panel hosts a wry child webview (edit mode of the vendored runtimes), synced with egui over IPC (ARCHITECTURE §4). Code editor is native (syntect highlighting + our own editing features); CodeMirror remains vendored only for the HTML manual&#39;s live examples. Mac wrapper uses a minimal wry + tao nord-player . Consequences. + fast, single Rust binary, native look, no web UI framework, easy cross-platform. − Must build a code editor on egui (no multi-cursor/LSP initially); − wry-in-egui embedding quirks (spike in M3-07; fallback = separate wry window); − editor UI tested via egui_kittest not Playwright. PCUI no longer vendored. ADR: Skins + themes are data (assets/themes/themes.json), dark only (2026-10-12) # A skin is a font pair (UI + code, ligatures on/off); a theme is a dark color set (UI colors + syntax colors). One JSON is the source for the JS runtime (generated module), the egui editor ( include_str! ) and the code editor. Light themes are deliberately not shipped for now. All fonts are vendored woff2 (OFL) — no CDN. ADR-014 Editor ⇄ viewport/game bridge is HTTP/SSE through nord-serve , not wry IPC (M3, 2026-10-12) # Context. The plan (ARCHITECTURE §4) used evaluate_script (down) and ipc_handler (up). That makes the whole editor loop depend on the macOS-only, unverifiable-in-sandbox webview embedding, and it can&#39;t be tested without a window. Decision. The dev server became a library crate nord-serve (used by nord serve and in-process by the editor). It owns a Hub : editor → page commands over SSE ( /__edit/events , also injectable via POST /__edit/cmd ), page → editor messages via POST /api/ipc , game console via POST /api/log (a tiny script injected into served HTML forwards console.* /errors). The embedded webview is then only a display surface for a URL. Consequences. + protocol fully tested headless (Playwright against the real nord serve ), identical in the Open in browser fallback; + the webview spike (M3-07) shrinks to"},{"f":"known-issues.html","t":"Known issues","s":"Appendix","x":"Format: BUG-nnn [open|fixed] title — repro / notes . Unverified-but-plausible risks are listed under Unverified . Open # (none yet) Fixed # BUG-009 [fixed] Assets folder tree snapped back to a narrow width and file tiles descended in a staircase. Disabled tree scroll-area auto-shrink; paint thumbnails/icons without advancing the already allocated cell&#39;s layout cursor. Headless regressions verify retained divider width and aligned/wrapped rows at 48/84/160 px. Native pointer interaction in the rebuilt app remains unverified. BUG-007 [fixed] macOS child webviews were vertically mirrored, covering the game header/console. wry 0.50 assumes an unflipped NSView but winit 0.30 supplies a flipped view; bounds now compensate. Reproduced and verified in the packaged editor with Noom on macOS, plus geometry regression at multiple zooms. BUG-008 [fixed] Selecting an entity silently inserted missing component/script defaults and dirtied the scene. Inspector now displays defaults locally and writes only fields the user edits; regression opens Noom and verifies an unchanged scene/undo stack. BUG-003 [fixed] Toolbar Export was permanently disabled despite the implemented export dialog; click regression now dispatches Export. BUG-004 [fixed] Embedded views reappeared above menus/dialogs because end_frame overrode set_visible(false); frame visibility now honors overlays, including newly opened popups and recovery/desktop dialogs. BUG-005 [fixed] Changing themes during Play could restore stale colors after Stop; the tint base is reset when applying a theme. BUG-006 [fixed] Packaged hub omitted starter templates; build.sh now ships templates/ alongside examples/. BUG-002 [fixed] macOS project locks misidentified live foreign-user processes as stale because kill -0 returned EPERM. The macOS check now reads ps -p PID -o pid= ; the existing exclusivity regression covers pid 1. BUG-001 [fixed] Rebuilt app examples showed old art/code — the hub reused persistent private copies under ~/.config/nord/examples/ . USE CACHED CONTENT now defaults OFF: current example files open directly and old Recent/launch paths resolve to the current bundle. Opt-in ON retains saved copies for testing. Regression replaces an example asset then reopens it and verifies the new bytes; server regression verifies no-store and new bytes at the same URL. Verification for M8-18 # Workspace tests pass with the editor webview feature (42 editor unit tests + 27 headless UI tests), Clippy all targets, formatting, task consistency, manual checks, and signed macOS app build. Native macOS checks: Graphite hub/workspace, packaged starter catalog, Noom Play/Stop, unchanged blue play tint, Settings above the webview, Export from the toolbar, and corrected viewport placement above the Console. Other native OS-specific paths and the broader owner checklist below remain unverified. Verification for M8-19 # Assets regressions pass for divider dragging, row alignment/wrapping at 48/84/160 px and loaded thumbnails. Workspace tests with webview pass with hub_search_opens_examples_and_docs_without_a_project run separately: the shared Recent history can otherwise give its existing noom label query two matching buttons. All-targets Clippy, formatting, manual checks and the signed macOS bundle build pass. Native Assets pointer interaction remains unverified. Unverified / risks # RISK-010 (M9, owner action) Desktop shell parts that are not verified on macOS/Windows : native menu bar, tray icon, global shortcuts, dock badge/taskbar progress ( HostCommand::Badge/Progress : badge macOS only, progress via tao), context menus on Windows/Linux (only macOS popup is wired), app.autostart (LaunchAgent/registry/.desktop writers), file-association/URL-scheme registration (Info.plist keys generated; Windows register.reg needs the install path edited), rfd / notify-rust / arboard backends. Linux (webkit2gtk under Xvfb) runs the IPC/files/store/server smoke test in CI; Windows cargo check of the shell passes, macOS cannot be checked here. CI runs tools/player-smoke.mjs on macOS/Windows as non-blocking steps. Wayland: tray/global shortcuts compositor-dependent. RISK-011 (M9) Permission manifest = application-level guard, not an OS sandbox (no App Sandbox/security-scoped bookmarks yet: picked paths are only remembered for the current run). Dialogs/ process.run / http.fetch run on the UI thread in the native player (a slow call blocks the window; the page itself is not blocked). dialog.open in the browser fallback copies the chosen file into virtual app data. Clipboard images are unsupported natively. Markdown code fences are not syntax-highlighted in the code editor (CodeMirror codeLanguages not in the vendored bundle). RISK-012 (M8) ./build.sh for Windows has never been run (CI job windows-latest is non-blocking); macOS notarisation steps in docs/PACKAGING.md follow Apple&#39;s docs but are untested here. RISK-001a (M3-07 owner action needed ) The webview feature compiles (type-checked against wry&#39;s Linux backend; the macOS build runs in CI macos-14 ) but embedding was never run on macOS. Checklist on the M1 Max: ./run.sh examples/conformance-2d → (1) Scene tab shows the 2D view in the panel rect, (2) resize the window/split → view follows, (3) Retina scale correct (no 2× offset), (4) click inside the view then press W/E/R/Cmd+Z → editor reacts (keys are forwarded), click back in egui → shortcuts work, (5) open a menu / Cmd+K → the webview pauses (placeholder) and returns, (6) ▶ Play shows the game in the Game tab, Stop clears it. If anything is off use ./run.sh --no-webview (Open in browser fallback; protocol identical). On Linux the webview is not wired (needs GTK loop) — macOS only for now. M3 editor verified here: egui_kittest (12 headless UI tests), Xvfb + software GL smoke run (splash → hub → project), Playwright for the viewport protocol/picking/drag/snap/gizmos/thumbnails/log bridge. Not verified: window-geometry restore (needs a window manager), drag-and-drop "},{"f":"changelog.html","t":"Changelog","s":"Appendix","x":"2026-10-12 (M8-18): Refreshed editor with neutral Graphite default, cleaner dock/toolbar/hub, searchable examples, scrolling settings, accessible icons and bundled starters. Fixed disabled Export, webview dialog overlap, hub Docs navigation, mirrored macOS viewport bounds, Inspector mutating on selection and theme restoration during Play; preserved PLAYMODE-COLOR. Newest first. One line per notable change. Credit: Crowelian 2026 + Sonnet. Unreleased # M6-27 X-KOM art redraw (2026-10-13): replaced all 31 PNG sheets with original 1990s X-COM / XCOM 2-inspired art: tactical terrain/objects/soldiers/Sectoids/Mutons, military and alien RTS units/buildings, interception craft/scenery, geoscape, pixel-exact cursors/FX/pickups/map markers/terminal font. Existing dimensions, frame order and metadata preserved; 23 reviewed raster masters + exact ImageGen prompts and eight drawn sheets re-pack offline. Mission mask now matches the world picture; HQ moved off the ocean; cards honor their requested color. Verified 117 occupied image/frame regions, distinct animations, exact regeneration, nine browser screens, tactical/RTS sims, CLI build and offline web export. M6-25/26 Example games X-KOM 2026 + Red Front 2026: examples/xkom = X-COM-style campaign (geoscape, base + 7-tech research, skippable UFO interception with radar animation + Star Fox-style shooter, procedural isometric turn-based missions with TU / reaction fire / fog of war from OpenXcom rules, and at 100 % alien presence a Red Alert RTS hive strike; win by kill count). examples/redfront = Red Alert-style RTS skirmish sharing the RTS engine (OpenRA stats, A*, harvesters, production, AI waves). Hand-pixelled art ( tools/art/xkom , tools/art/redfront ), synthesized audio ( tools/gen_xkom_audio.mjs ), assets.md + GUIDE.md in both. Follow-up: F1/H in-game help (all modes), Star Fox/Raptor-style interception shooter (banking, barrel roll that reflects shots, P/M/S pickups, homing missiles, darts, ground turrets, 2-phase boss), vendored Google fonts (VT323 + Orbitron) and bevelled X-COM-style panels. Sims: node tools/xk-sim.mjs , node tools/rts-sim.mjs [xkom|redfront] ; screenshots: node tools/xk-shots.mjs [xkom|redfront] → docs/screenshots/ . M8-19 Assets layout: folder-tree divider keeps the dragged width instead of shrinking back to its labels; thumbnails and file/folder icons paint inside their allocated cells so the grid forms aligned rows instead of a staircase. Headless regressions cover divider dragging and three thumbnail sizes. Editor storage locations: manual documents exact macOS, Windows and Linux settings/saved-example paths, NORD_CONFIG_DIR , Windows&#39;s working-directory fallback when HOME is unset, and project thumbnail/autosave/lock locations. macOS project-lock fix: check process existence with ps instead of treating kill -0 permission failures as a dead process; the existing pid-1 exclusivity regression now passes. M8-16 current editor content / BUG-001: Settings → USE CACHED CONTENT defaults OFF. Examples/Tutorials open current checkout/bundle files; old saved-copy Recent/launch paths resolve to the current example. ON opts into persistent private copies for testing. Webviews use temporary storage by default and recreate on mode changes; script attributes bypass caching when OFF. HTTP project files already use no-store (now regression-tested with changed bytes at the same URL). Docs/manual updated. M6-20 RetroPaxDungeons art redraw: all eleven tile, hero/monster, inventory, effect, HUD and font sheets refreshed with original retro dungeon pixel art (built-in ImageGen + hand-drawn effects/shared dungeon font). Frame layouts and animation/item IDs preserved; reviewed raster masters + prompts re-pack offline from tools/art/rpd/redrawn/ . Verified 66 regions, font layout, transparency, baselines, repeating terrain edges, distinct idle frames, exact regeneration, 40-seed simulation, web export and live browser movement with no reported errors. Attribution URLs moved from JS comments to documentation so offline export passes. M6-19 Noom art redraw: all ten monster, prop/effect, weapon, wall/surface, HUD and font sheets replaced with original Barony/Doom-inspired pixel artwork (built-in ImageGen + hand-drawn lettering). Existing frame names, sizes and atlas rectangles preserved; reviewed raster masters + prompts stored under tools/art/noom/redrawn/ and re-pack offline. Verified 88 sprite/HUD regions, 12 tiles, transparency, baselines, repeating edges, exact regeneration, simulation, web export and live browser rendering. Standalone Playwright smoke unavailable locally (Chromium absent); full export URL scanner still reports existing Babylon vendor defaults (KNOWN_ISSUES). Docs tab no longer embeds the HTML manual (the native webview floated above all panels): it shows an Open the manual in your browser button plus the Markdown spec reader. Reset: View ▸ Reset layout, palette entries, and Settings ▸ Reset panel layout / Reset all settings to defaults (keeps window size). Test reset_settings_and_layout_restore_defaults . Game view offset = real move: x/y translate the view by exactly that many pixels with its size unchanged (anchor picks the start in the free space; only the window edge limits it, so it may overlap neighbouring panels when you push it). Replaces the shrink/crop behaviour. Game view offset moves instead of cropping: x/y offsets now set where the view starts (the free area shrinks by the offset, the game is scaled to fit it with its aspect kept) — no more cut-off. Test game_view_y_offset_moves_not_crops . Game view position works in Fit mode: Fit now scales the game to the largest aspect-correct size inside the panel (was: fill the panel, leaving no room to move), so Position/offsets take effect; offsets push the view and it is cropped at the panel edge. Vertical Top/Middle/Bottom only differ when the view is shorter than the panel (pick 50–100 % or a wide game). Tests game_view_offset_moves_in_fit_mode . Play → Stop → "}];